hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"13916","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The dry measure is upon the dry measure of Mecca, and the scale is upon the scale of Medina. [wording as in source; cf. 13917]"
"13917","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah, who said: The Prophet said: The dry measure is the dry measure of the people of Medina, and the weight is the weight of the people of Mecca. From al-Thawri, from a man, from 'Ata', from the Prophet, the same."
"13918","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, who said: Ibn 'Umar passed by a man measuring, as if exceeding in it, and said to him: Woe to you, what is this? He said to him: Allah commanded fulfillment. Ibn 'Umar said: And He forbade aggression."
"13919","Ma'mar reported, he said: I saw the mudd of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with Isma'il ibn Umayya; I deem it a ratl and a half. He said: And I know only that I tested it and found it three quarters of a quarter. [measure obscure in source]"
"13920","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il, from Mahan, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: He passed by a man weighing [gap in source] who had given preponderance, so 'Abd Allah overturned the scale and said: What a good tongue, then add after that what you wish. [source has a small gap]"
"13921","From al-Thawri, from Simmak ibn Harb, from Suwayd ibn Qays: I and Makhrafah al-Abdi brought cloth from Hajr, and we came with it to Makkah, then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came to us at Mina and bargained with us for trousers, and bought them from us. He said: and there was a weigher there who weighed for a wage, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Weigh and give extra."
"13922","Al-Thawri informed us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: There is no harm in giving extra in weighing."
"13923","Al-Aslami informed us, from Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, borrowed silver from a man, and when he repaid him he placed the silver in the scale pan and it outweighed, so it was said: You have given extra. Then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: That is how we weigh."
"13924","Al-Thawri informed us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, and from Qatadah, from al-Hasan; al-Thawri said it and al-Hasan said it: concerning a sword containing ornament, and a belt, and a ring, then you buy it for more or less, or on credit, and he saw no harm in it."
"13925","Al-Thawri said: And our saying is: If he sells it for more than what is in it, there is no harm in it."
"13926","And likewise Ibn al-Taymi informed us, from Nadrah, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: If the ornament is less than the price, there is no harm in it. He said: And Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah informed me, from al-Shabi, the like of the hadith of Mughirah, from Ibrahim."
"13927","Al-Thawri informed us, from al-Hajjaj, from Jafar ibn Amr ibn Hurayth, from his father, that Ali sold Amr ibn Hurayth a decorated coat of mail for four thousand dirhams until the stipend, or until something else, and the stipend at that time had a known term."
"13928","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, and Qatadah, from Ibn Sirin: that they both disliked that a ring containing a stone be sold for silver."
"13929","Al-Thawri informed us, from Ibn Sirin: that he disliked it."
"13930","From al-Thawri, from Abd Allah ibn Abi al-Safar, from al-Shabi, he said: A man came and asked us, so we said: Go to this man! And they pointed to Shurayh. So he came to him and said: From whom are you? He said: From those upon whom Allah has bestowed favor, and my lineage is among Kindah. He said: Then the Bedouin returned to them and said: You are mocking me. He said: Then he asked him about a collar of gold containing stones and jewels, so he said: Remove the collar and sell it weight for weight, and sell the jewels however you wish."
"13931","Hushaym informed us, from Mughirah, he said: I asked Ibrahim about a ring: Shall I sell it on credit? He said: Does it contain stones? I said: Yes. He said: Then as if he hesitated about it."
"13932","Abu Sufyan informed us, from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas, he said: The letter of Umar came to us while we were in the land of Persia. He said: Do not sell anything containing silver inlay for silver."
"13933","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, and Ibn Umar, they both said: Whoever has a claim upon a man until a known term, then takes part of it early and leaves part of it for him, then it is usury. Mamar said: And I do not know anyone before us except that he disliked it."
"13934","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Dhakwan, from Busr ibn Said, from Abu Salih, the freedman of al-Saffah, he said: I sold cloth until a term, then my companions offered me that they would pay me early and I would reduce for them, so I asked Zayd ibn Thabit about that and he said: Do not consume it and do not feed it to others."
"13935","Hisham informed us, from al-Hasan, and Muhammad, if Allah wills, that they both disliked it, and they said: There is no harm in your taking goods if you wish to take early."
"13936","From al-Thawri, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, he said: I asked Ibn al-Musayyib about that, and he said: Those are dirhams, the prompt for the deferred. Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Dawud, from Ibn al-Musayyib, the like of it."
"13937","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: Abu al-Minhal Abd al-Rahman ibn Mutim informed me, he said: I asked Ibn Umar about a man against whom I have a claim until a term, and I said: Pay me early and I will reduce for you. So he forbade me from it, and said: The Commander of the Believers forbade us to sell cash for debt."
"13938","Mamar informed us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas: He was asked about a man who has a claim upon a man until a term, then he says: Pay me early and I will reduce for you. So he said: There is no harm in that. From al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: Ibn Abbas was asked about that and saw no harm in it. Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Amr, from Ibn Abbas, the like of it."
"13939","Ibn Uyaynah said: And one other than Amr informed me, he said: Ibn Abbas said: Usury is only: Delay for me and I will increase you, and it is not: Pay me early and I will reduce for you."
"13940","Al-Thawri informed us, from Hammad, and Mansur, from Ibrahim, concerning a man who has a claim until a term and he says: Pay me early and I will reduce for you; he saw no harm in it."
"13941","Sufyan said: And we see no harm in his taking goods, and we do not know anyone who disliked it except Ibn Umar."
"13942","Al-Aslami informed us, from a shaykh of the people of Madinah, that Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, made a contract with a mukatab of hers for gold or silver."
"13943","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from Bakr ibn Abd Allah al-Muzani, from Ibn Umar: that he saw no harm in goods taken from the mukatab. And the like of it from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz."
"13944","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas: that he was asked about the mukatab, that reduction is made for him and collection is taken early from him, and he saw no harm in it; and Ibn Umar disliked it, except in goods."
"13945","Israel informed us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rafi, from Qays, the freedman of Ibn Yamin, he said: I asked Ibn Umar and said: We go out with trade to the land of Basrah and to Syria, and we sell on credit, then we wish to depart, so they say: Reduce for us and we will pay you in cash. He said: This one is commanding me to give him a fatwa to consume usury and to feed it. And he seized my upper arm three times. So I said: I am only asking you for a fatwa. He said: Then do not do it."
"13946","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, he said: I said to al-Shabi: Ibrahim said, concerning a man who has a debt upon a man, that he reduces part of it for him and takes part of it early, that there is no harm in it; but al-Hakam ibn Utaybah disliked it. So al-Shabi said: Al-Hakam is right, and Ibrahim is wrong."
"13947","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, he said: A man came to him and said: This one owes me a claim until a term, then my family came and demanded it from them, and took it before its due time. So Shurayh said: Return it to him so that he may benefit from it to the extent you benefited from it."
"13948","Al-Thawri informed us concerning a man who sold a man a thousand garments, then nine hundred and ninety-nine were found and one garment was missing. He said: The sale is rejected, because he does not know how much the value of that garment is."
"13949","Al-Thawri said concerning a man who sold a garment and said: I sell you this garment, and its fulling is upon me, or its stitching is upon me. He said: It is disliked and rejected, because he has bought a sale and a burden together; then if the garment is stolen while with the buyer, it is from the wealth of the seller."
"13950","Mamar informed us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: that he disliked that milk be bought while in the udders of sheep."
"13951","Al-Thawri informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Do not buy milk while in the udders of sheep, nor wool upon their backs."
"13952","Yahya ibn al-Ala informed us, from Jahdam ibn Abd Allah, from Muhammad ibn Zayd, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade the sale of spoils of war until they are divided, and the sale of charities until they are received, and the sale of a runaway slave, and the sale of what is in the bellies of livestock until they give birth, and what is in their udders except by measure, and the diver's strike."
"13953","Mamar informed us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, and Jabir ibn Zayd, they both said: There is no usury between a slave and his master."
"13954","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, from al-Shaybani, and al-Shabi, they both said: There is no usury between a slave and his master."
"13955","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Mabad, the freedman of Ibn Abbas, he said: Ibn Abbas used to sell fruit from a slave of his before its ripeness appeared, and he used to say: There is no usury between a slave and his master."
"13956","Al-Thawri said: It is disliked that you sell your mukatab two dirhams for a dirham. He said: And if the mukatab steals something from his master, his hand is not cut; and if the master steals something from the mukatab, his hand is not cut."
"13957","A shaykh of the people of Taif called Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman informed us, he said: I heard Amr ibn al-Sharid narrating, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The neighbor has more right to his adjoining property."
"13958","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Amr ibn al-Sharid, that Abu Rafi was bargained with by Sad for a house of his, so Sad said to him: I will not increase you beyond four hundred mithqals. Abu Rafi said: Had I not heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: The neighbor has more right to his adjoining property, I would not have given it to you."
"13959","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Amr ibn al-Sharid al-Thaqafi, he said: Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah placed one of his hands upon my shoulder, then we set out until we came to Sad. Then Abu Rafi came and said to al-Miswar: Will you not command this one to buy from me? So Sad said: By Allah, I will not increase you beyond this, beyond four hundred dinars, either in a lump or in installments. So Abu Rafi said: Glory be to Allah! I was offered five hundred in cash for it; and had I not heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: The neighbor has more right to his adjoining property, I would not have given it to you."
"13960","Al-Thawri informed us, from Mansur, from al-Hasan, from one who heard Ali, and Ibn Masud, saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, judged by neighborhood."
"13961","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Rashid, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, judged by neighborhood."
"13962","Mamar informed us, from Qatadah, and al-Hasan, they both said: If he is his attached neighbor, then he has preemption."
"13963","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from al-Shabi, and Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, he said: The partner has more right than the preemptor, and the preemptor has more right than anyone besides him. From al-Thawri, from Ashath, from al-Shabi, from Shurayh, the like of it. Hisham informed us, from Muhammad, from Shurayh, the like of it."
"13964","Al-Thawri informed us, from al-Hasan ibn Ubayd Allah, from Fudayl, from Ibrahim, he said: The partner has more right than the neighbor, and the neighbor has more right than others."
"13965","Abu Sufyan informed us, from Hisham ibn al-Mughirah, he said: I heard al-Shabi saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The preemptor has more right than the neighbor, and the neighbor has more right than the distant one."
"13966","He said: Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, made preemption only in whatever has not been divided; so when the boundaries are fixed and the roads are turned aside, then there is no preemption."
"13967","Al-Thawri informed us, and Ibn Jurayj, from Yahya ibn Said, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: When land is divided and the boundaries are fixed, there is no preemption in it."
"13968","Malik informed us, from Muhammad ibn Umarah, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, that Uthman ibn Affan said: When the boundaries fall, there is no preemption in it, and there is no preemption in a well nor in a stallion."
"13969","Mamar informed us, and al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz said: When the boundaries are struck, there is no preemption."
"13970","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, he said: I said to Tawus: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote it: When the boundaries are struck there is no preemption? So Tawus said: No, the neighbor has more right."
"13971","Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman informed us, from Ata, from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The neighbor has more right to his preemption; he is waited for if he is absent, when their road is one."
"13972","Al-Thawri informed us, from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from Humayd al-Azraq, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz judged by it after fourteen years."
"13973","Al-Thawri informed us, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, and al-Hakam, they both said: The absent one has preemption."
"13974","Mamar informed us, and Numan, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: Preemption is by neighborhood, and it is by doors."
"13975","Al-Thawri informed us, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, from Ibrahim al-Nakhai, he said: Preemption is by doors."
"13976","Jafar ibn Abi Sulayman informed us, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, from Talhah ibn Abd Allah ibn Awf, from Aishah, she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I have two lady neighbors, so to which of them shall I give a gift? He said: To the nearer of them to you in door."
"13977","Al-Thawri informed us, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from Shurayh, he said: He used to judge for the first neighbor then the first, meaning the walls."
"13978","From al-Thawri, and Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever has a partnership in land or buildings, it is not lawful for him to sell until he seeks permission of his partner; if he wishes he takes it, and if he wishes he leaves it."
"13979","Al-Thawri informed us, from Ashath, from al-Hakam, concerning two men between whom is a house or land, and one of them says to his companion: I wish to sell you the preemption, so buy from me; and he says: The price has been fixed, so I have more right. He said: He has nothing when he has given permission. Al-Thawri said: And by it we take. He said: And Ibn Abi Layla said: Preemption does not fall due for him until the sale occurs; if he wishes he takes, and if he wishes he leaves."
"13980","Abu Sufyan informed us, from Yunus, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Shabi, he said: Whoever has his preemption sold while he is present and does not deny it, then his preemption has gone."
"13981","From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from a man, from Shurayh, he said: Preemption is only for one who pursues it. And it is the saying of Mamar."
"13982","Al-Thawri said: We heard that preemption is not sold, nor gifted, nor inherited, nor lent; and it belongs to its owner to whom it fell due. Fudayl informed us, from Muhammad ibn Salim, from al-Shabi, the like of it."
"13983","Al-Thawri informed us, from al-Shaybani, from al-Shabi, he said: If he builds it then the preemptor comes afterwards, then the value is due. And Hammad said: This one uproots his building, and this one takes his preemption from the land. And the saying of Hammad is more beloved to al-Thawri."
"13984","Al-Thawri informed us concerning a man who bought a house for a thousand dirhams, then the preemptor came and the house was valued after he had sold its door for a thousand dirhams. He said: The preemptor takes the door for five hundred dirhams."
"13985","Al-Thawri informed us, from Humayd al-Tawil, from al-Hasan or Anas, I am the one in doubt, he said: The disbeliever has no preemption. And others of our companions said: He has preemption."
"13986","Qays ibn al-Rabi informed us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote: That the Jew has preemption."
"13987","Al-Thawri said: Preemption is for the old and the young, and the Bedouin, and the Jew, and the Christian, and the Magian. Then if he knows for three days and does not seek it, then he has no preemption. And if he tarries days then seeks it and says: I did not know I had preemption, then he is suspect."
"13988","Al-Thawri informed us, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: The Bedouin has no preemption. And al-Hakam said: He has preemption."
"13989","From al-Thawri, from Ashath, from al-Shabi, he said: Preemption is upon the heads of men."
"13990","Al-Hasan ibn Umarah said, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim: Preemption is upon the heads of men."
"13991","Al-Thawri said, from a companion of his, from Ibrahim, from Shurayh, he said: It is upon the shares."
"13992","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Preemption is by the shares."
"13993","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Preemption is by the shares."
"13994","I asked Mamar about two men between whom is an undivided ruin, then one of them sold his share of that ruin, and sold with it another ruin of his for one price, then the preemptor came and said: I take his share of the ruin. He said: Uthman al-Batti said: He takes the whole sale or leaves it whole. And Ibn Shubrumah and others of the people of Kufah said: He takes half of the ruin that is between him and his companion at the value, and leaves the other if he wishes. I heard al-Thawri, and Sufyan, saying the like of the saying of Ibn Shubrumah."
"13995","I heard al-Thawri, and he was asked about a man who sold a man land containing preemption for another man until a term, then the preemptor came and said: I take it until its term. He said: He does not take it except in cash, because it has entered the liability of the first. He said: And among us are those who say: It is left in the hand of the one who bought it; then when the term arrives, the preemptor takes it."
"13996","Mamar informed us, he said: I said to Ayyub: Do you know anyone who placed preemption in animals? He said: No. Mamar said: And I do not know anyone who places preemption in animals."
"13997","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from Shurayh, he said: There is no preemption except in real estate or land."
"13998","From al-Thawri, he said: Israel informed us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah: There is no preemption except in land."
"13999","And Ibn Abi Mulaykah said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, judged with preemption in everything."
"14000","Malik informed us, from Muhammad ibn Umarah, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, that Uthman ibn Affan said: When the boundaries fall in land, there is no preemption in it, and there is no preemption in a well nor in a stallion."
"14001","Ibn Abi Sabrah informed us, from Muhammad ibn Umarah, from Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: There is no preemption in water, nor in a road, nor in a stallion, meaning the palm."
"14002","Al-Aslami informed us, from Abu Tuwalah, from Aban ibn Uthman ibn Affan, he said: There is no preemption in a well, nor in a stallion."
"14003","Mamar informed us, from Ibn Shubrumah, that he said: In water there is preemption. Mamar said: So what he said did not please me."
"14004","Israel informed us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The partner is a preemptor in everything."
"14005","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: I did not see the judges except that they judge: Whoever buys a debt against a man, then the owner of the debt has more right to it."
"14006","Mamar informed us, from a man of Quraysh, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz judged concerning a mukatab who bought what was upon him for goods, so he made the mukatab have more right to himself; then he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever buys a debt upon a man, then the owner of the debt has more right when he pays the like of what his companion paid."
"14007","Al-Aslami informed us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr informed me, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, judged with preemption in debt, and it is a man selling a debt of his upon a man, so the owner of the debt is more entitled to it."
"14008","Ibn Saman informed us, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: There is no preemption in animals."
"14009","Al-Thawri said concerning a man who bought land, then a man came and said: Half of it is mine, and the partner said: No; then he disputed him afterwards and established it. He said: He has preemption, because his right was established afterwards. Then a man said to him: Malik said: He has nothing except that he testifies at the time of his dispute to his prompt claim. But he refused."
"14010","Ismail ibn Abd Allah informed us, from Ubayd Allah ibn al-Hasan, a judge of the people of Basrah: that he judged that when a man buys a thing in which another has preemption, and the buyer takes possession, then the preemptor comes and takes it by his preemption from his hand, then the liability is for him upon the buyer; but if the buyer has not taken possession and the preemptor takes it from the first seller, then the liability is for him upon the first seller."
"14011","From al-Thawri, from Aslam, from Ata, he said: A term is not sold for a term."
"14012","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from al-Hakam, he said: A term is not sold for a term. Al-Thawri said: And its explanation with us is that he says: Give me tonight such and such, and I will give you after tomorrow the dirhams."
"14013","From al-Thawri, from Kulayb ibn Wail, he said: I asked Ibn Umar about a man against whom are dirhams; shall I sell them to him for food? He said: No."
"14014","Al-Aslami informed us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade the sale of kali, and it is the sale of debt for debt; and the sale of majr, and it is the sale of what is in the bellies of camels; and shighar."
"14015","Al-Thawri said: If he has advanced part of it on credit, and part of it in cash, then it is all invalid."
"14016","Al-Thawri said: And if you advance a hundred dirhams for a hundred faraq until a term, saying: I pay you now fifty, and fifty in a month, then the whole sale is invalid, because the contract is one."
"14017","Al-Thawri said: There is no advance except with taking possession, and surety in it is nothing."
"14018","Mamar informed us, from Qatadah ibn al-Musayyib, and Salim ibn Abd Allah, and Ibrahim al-Nakhai: that they saw no harm in leasing land; they used to lease their lands."
"14019","Mamar informed us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father: He saw no harm in leasing land."
"14020","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri: that he saw no harm in leasing land for gold and silver, and he disliked it for food, and he said: It is muhaqalah."
"14021","Mamar informed us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, he said: I said to Said ibn Jubayr that Ikrimah claims that leasing land is not proper. So he said: Ikrimah has lied. I heard Ibn Abbas saying: The best of what you do with white land is that you lease white land for gold and silver."
"14022","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Said, that Ibn Abbas said: The most proper of what you do is that you hire white land."
"14023","From al-Thawri, from Kulayb ibn Wail, he said: I asked Ibn Umar, I said: How do you see buying land? When I said: They take from every harvest a qafiz and a dirham, he said: Do not place littleness upon your neck."
"14024","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, and Said ibn Jubayr, that they both said: There is no harm in leasing white land."
"14025","From al-Thawri, from Yala ibn Ata, from al-Qasim ibn Abd Allah, he said: I asked Sad ibn Malik about leasing white land, and he said: There is no harm in it; that is the loan of land."
"14026","From al-Thawri, from Rabiah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Hanzalah ibn Qays, he said: I asked Rafi ibn Khadij about leasing white land, and he said: Lawful, no harm in it. Only irmadh was forbidden, that a man gives land and excepts part of it, and the like of that."
"14027","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Hanzalah ibn Qays al-Zuraqi, he said: I heard Rafi ibn Khadij saying: We were the most field-owning of the Ansar, and we used to lease land; then sometimes it would produce once, and not produce once, so we were forbidden from that; but as for for silver, we were not forbidden from it."
"14028","Mamar informed us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar used to lease his land, then he was informed of the hadith of Rafi ibn Khadij, so he informed him of it, and said: I knew that the people of land used to give their lands in the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and the owner of the land would stipulate that for him are the water channels, and what the spring waters, and he would stipulate something known from the threshing floor. He said: So Ibn Umar used to think that the prohibition was for what they used to stipulate."
"14029","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Salim ibn Abd Allah saying: Rafi ibn Khadij has been excessive upon himself. By Allah, we shall surely lease it as camels are leased; meaning that he was excessive in that he narrated from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that he forbade it, so we do not accept from him."
"14030","Muhammad ibn Muslim informed us, and Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to Uthman ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Suwayd: that he sell the whiteness of land for gold, and that he sharecrop upon the root of land."
"14031","Al-Thawri informed us, from Ibrahim al-Nakhai: that he hired white land until a known term for gold or silver."
"14032","Al-Thawri informed us, from Abd Allah ibn Isa, from Musa ibn Abd Allah ibn Yazid, he said: Ibn Umar was asked about leasing land, and said: My land and my camel are alike."
"14033","Al-Thawri said, and Ismail ibn Abi Khalid informed us, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: My land and my wealth are alike."
"14034","Mamar informed us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: that he used to dislike white land."
"14035","Mamar informed us, from one who heard al-Hasan, and Ata: They both disliked it too."
"14036","Malik informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade muzabanah and muhaqalah. And muzabanah is buying dates for dates, and muhaqalah is buying crops for wheat, and hiring land for wheat."
"14037","Malik said, al-Zuhri said: So I asked Ibn al-Musayyib about hiring it for gold and silver, and he said: There is no harm in it."
"14038","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Usayd ibn Zahir, the nephew of Rafi ibn Khadij, he said: When one of us had no need of his land, he would give it for a third and a fourth and a half, and stipulate a third of the brooks, and the fulling, and what the spring waters. And living at that time was hard, and we used to work in it with iron and with what Allah willed, and we obtained benefit from it. Then Rafi ibn Khadij came and said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, has forbidden you a matter that was beneficial, and obedience to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, is more beneficial for you. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbids you haql, and says: Whoever has no need of his land, let him lend it to his brother, or let him leave it; and he forbids muzabanah. And muzabanah is that a man has great wealth of palms, then a man comes to him and says: I have taken it for such and such measure of dates."
"14039","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from al-Zuraqi, from Rafi ibn Khadij, he said: My maternal uncle came to me one day and said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, has forbidden us today a matter that was beneficial for you, and obedience to Allah and His Messenger is more beneficial for us and more beneficial for you. And he passed by crops and said: Whose is this? They said: So-and-so's. He said: Whose is the land? They said: So-and-so's. He said: Then what is the affair of this one? They said: He gave it to him for such and such. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: That one of you lend his brother is better for him than that he take upon it a known rent. And he forbade a third and a fourth, and leasing land."
"14040","Ayyub said: So it was said to Tawus: Here is a son of Rafi ibn Khadij narrating this hadith. So he entered then came out and said: One more knowledgeable than this one has narrated to me: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, only passed by crops and it pleased him, so he said: Whose is this? They said: So-and-so's. He said: Then whose is the land? They said: So-and-so's. He said: And how? They said: He gave it to him for such and such. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: That one of you lend his brother is better for him. And he did not forbid it."
"14041","Al-Thawri said: From Nasr Abu Juzay, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from al-Walid, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that he said: May Allah forgive Rafi ibn Khadij. By Allah, this hadith was not like this. That man had only leased land to a man, then they fought and reviled each other over a matter they disputed in, so the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: If this is your affair, then do not lease land. So Rafi heard the end of the hadith, and did not hear its beginning."
"14042","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I said to Tawus: If only you left mukhabarah, for they claim that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade it. So he said: O Amr! The most knowledgeable of them informed me, meaning Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not forbid it."
"14043","Mamar informed us, and Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: That one of you lend his brother his land is better for him than that he take upon it such and such for a known thing. He said: And Ibn Abbas said: It is haql, and it is in the tongue of the Ansar muhaqalah."
"14044","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave Khaybar to the Jews to work it with half of it for them. So it continued upon that: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and Abu Bakr, and two years of the caliphate of Umar, until Umar expelled them from it."
"14045","Mamar informed us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi: that Khaybar, which the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, shared, contained crops and palms; so he used to divide for his wives every year from it a hundred wasqs, eighty wasqs of dates, and twenty wasqs of barley for a wife."
"14046","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, from Musa ibn Talhah, he said: Uthman granted to five of the companions of Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: to Abd Allah, and to Sad, and to al-Zubayr, and to Khabbab, and to Usamah ibn Zayd. Then my two neighbors, Abd Allah and Sad, used to give their lands for a third."
"14047","From al-Thawri, from al-Harith ibn Hasirah, he said: Sakhr ibn al-Walid narrated to me, from Amr ibn Sulay al-Muharibi, he said: A man came to Ali and informed against a man, saying: He has taken land and does with it such and such. So the man said: I have taken it for half; I dig its rivers, and repair it, and inhabit it. So Ali said: No harm. And digging rivers is hollowing them."
"14048","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from Muhammad ibn Zayd, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent me to the villages of Arbah, and commanded me to take the share of land. Sufyan said: And its share is a third and a fourth, and he saw no harm in it."
"14049","Mamar informed us, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about a man giving his land for a third and a fourth. He said: No harm in it. Mamar said: And one who asked al-Qasim ibn Muhammad about it informed me, and he saw no harm in it."
"14050","I heard Hisham narrating, he said: Muhammad ibn Sirin sent me to al-Qasim ibn Muhammad to ask him about a man who says to another: Work in this garden of mine and for you is a third or a fourth. So he said: No harm in it. He said: So I returned to Ibn Sirin and informed him, and he said: This is the best of what is done in land. Hisham said: And al-Hasan used to dislike it."
"14051","Al-Thawri informed us, from Hammad, he said: I asked Ibrahim, and Ibn al-Musayyib, and Said ibn Jubayr, and Mujahid, about a third and a fourth, and they disliked it."
"14052","Al-Thawri said: And Qays ibn Muslim informed me, from Abu Jafar, he said: There is no house of emigration in Madinah except they give their lands for a third and a fourth."
"14053","Abu Sufyan informed us, he said: Amr ibn Uthman ibn Mawhib informed me, he said: I heard Abu Jafar Muhammad ibn Ali saying: The family of Abu Bakr, and the family of Umar, and the family of Ali give their lands for a third and a fourth."
"14054","I asked Mamar about a man who worked in his land a work, then it occurred to him to share it with a man and return that work to him; so he disliked it."
"14055","Al-Thawri informed us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, he said: Ibn Umar used to give his land for a third."
"14056","Mamar said: And al-Zuhri saw no harm in partnership."
"14057","Mamar informed us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: that he used to share his land upon a third and a half, and give them their share of the seed."
"14058","Mamar informed us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote: that they share land upon half, and do not guarantee the partners the seed."
"14059","Ismail ibn Abd Allah informed us, he said: More than one informed us, that Ibn Sirin used to share his land, and advance the seed to the partners, until he takes it afterwards from the crop of the land, when it is harvested."
"14060","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to the people of Najran: I have enjoined upon Yala good for whoever of you has accepted Islam, and I have commanded him to give half of what is worked of land; and I do not wish to expel you from it so long as you reform and are content with your work."
"14061","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Amir ibn Abd Allah ibn Nistas informed me, from Khaybar, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, conquered it, and it was a gathering to him, its tillage and its palms. He said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and his companions had no slaves, so the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, made peace with the Jews upon that: you suffice us the work, and for you is half the dates, upon that I settle you as long as it appears good to Allah and His Messenger. So that is when the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent Ibn Rawahah to assess between them. Then when he gave them choice, the Jews took the dates. So Khaybar remained in the hands of the Jews upon the peace of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, until Umar came, and expelled them. So the Jews said: Has not the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, made peace with us upon such and such? So he said: Rather upon that he settles you therein as long as it appears good to Allah and His Messenger; so this is when it has appeared good to me to expel you. So he expelled them, then he divided it among the Muslims who conquered it with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he did not give from it anyone who was not present at its conquest. So its people now are the Muslims; there are no Jews in it. Ibn Jurayj said: And Abd Allah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr informed me, from the settlement of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, with the people of Khaybar upon that: for us is half the dates and for you is half, and you suffice us the work."
"14062","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, and Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, permitted the sale of araya to be sold by its estimate, and did not permit in other than it; and the araya are those that are eaten. Al-Thawri said: If he buys fruit, then another bears fruit afterwards, then for him is what came out the first time."
"14063","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade muhaqalah and muzabanah. And muhaqalah is that he buys crops for wheat, and muzabanah is that he buys dates from the tops of palms for dates, and hiring land for wheat."
"14064","From al-Thawri, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, from Umar ibn Abi Salamah, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade muzabanah and muhaqalah. And muzabanah is dates for dates, and muhaqalah is wheat for wheat."
"14065","Malik informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade muzabanah. And muzabanah is selling dates for dates by measure, and selling fruit for raisins by measure."
"14066","Ma'mar reported from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: Surplus water is not to be withheld so that surplus pasture is thereby withheld."
"14067","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever withholds surplus water, Allah will withhold His bounty from him on the Day of Resurrection."
"14068","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever withholds surplus water in order to thereby withhold surplus pasture, Allah the Exalted will withhold His bounty from him on the Day of Resurrection."
"14069","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Rijal, from 'Amrah bint 'Abd al-Rahman, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade withholding the remaining water of a well."
"14070","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Surplus water is not to be withheld so that surplus pasture is thereby withheld."
"14071","Ibn 'Uyaynah reported from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu al-Minhal, who said: I heard Iyas ibn 'Abd saying: Do not withhold water, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbid the sale of water."
"14072","Ibn 'Uyaynah reported from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn al-Muntashir, from his father, who said: Masruq liked that waterskins from the Euphrates be bought for him so he could sell them and give their price in charity. He said: And Ibn Jurayj asked 'Ata about a man who carries water, may he sell it? He said: There is no harm; he carried it and exerted effort in it."
"14073","Sa'id ibn al-Sa'ib ibn Yasar reported, saying: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah informed me that it reached him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the sale of surplus water, and the leap of the camel, meaning thereby the fee for covering."
"14074","From al-Thawri, from Abu Mu'adh, who said: Al-Bara ibn 'Azib forbade me, though I am not a tayyas, and he said: The stud fee of the stallion is not lawful."
"14075","Ma'mar reported from Qatadah: He disliked the stud fee for the one who takes it, and saw no harm for the one who gives it."
"14076","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: A man asked him, saying: There are trees in my land, may I sell them? He said: No, but reserve them for your animals."
"14077","Ma'mar informed me, saying: One who heard 'Ikrimah informed me, saying: Do not consume the price of the tree, for it is ill-gotten gain, meaning pasture."
"14078","Ibrahim ibn 'Umar reported from 'Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah, from al-Hasan, that he used to dislike the sale of all pasture, whether meadow, plain, or mountain."
"14079","From al-Thawri, from 'Abdullah ibn Abi al-Safar, who said: I heard al-Sha'bi being asked about a man who buys the receipt (sakk) for wheat. He said: It is uncertainty (gharar); he is entitled to the value of his goods in cash. Al-Thawri said: And Ibn Abi Layla used to say: If he is brought together with its owner and he acknowledges what is in the receipt, then it is permissible."
"14080","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who was asked about selling wheat for the receipt and considered it permissible if he intended it, and if he did not intend it he has no recourse against its owner."
"14081","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn 'Abdullah being asked about a man to whom a debt is owed, may he buy a slave with it? He said: There is no harm in it."
"14082","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, and from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, both saying: The sale involving uncertainty (gharar) is forbidden. And sometimes Ma'mar raised the hadith of Ibn Abi Najih to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"14083","Ibn 'Uyaynah reported from Mujahid that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the sale involving uncertainty (gharar)."
"14084","Al-Aslami reported from Abu al-Zinad, from Ibn al-Musayyab, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the sale involving uncertainty (gharar)."
"14085","He said: And Husayn ibn Dumayrah informed me, from his father, from his grandfather, from 'Ali, that he used to forbid the sale involving uncertainty (gharar)."
"14086","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from 'Umar ibn Qudamah, that a man brought coconuts from Basra to Kufa and sold them, and some of them were found spoiled, so they disputed before Shurayh, and he said: Cheating is not permissible."
"14087","Ma'mar reported, saying: I asked Yahya ibn Abi Kathir about the sale of mines, saying: I have not heard anything about it, and he said: It is disliked, or they dislike it."
"14088","Ma'mar reported, saying: I heard Yahya ibn Abi Kathir disliking that a man buy the hide of the bull while it is standing."
"14089","From al-Thawri, who said: It is disliked that he sell the hide of the cow while it is standing, or its meat while it is standing."
"14090","Al-Thawri said: If he buys from you whatever is in this house, whatever it amounts to, each kurr for such-and-such, it is disliked until he says: Buy one hundred kurr for such-and-such."
"14091","I heard al-Thawri, when asked about a man who says to a man: Sell me half your house adjoining my house. He said: This is a rejected sale, because he does not know where his purchase ends; but if he said: I sell you half the house, or a quarter of the house, it is permissible. 'Abd al-Razzaq said: So I mentioned it to Ma'mar, and he said: This is all the same; there is no harm in it."
"14092","Ma'mar reported, saying: I asked al-Zuhri about the sale of mushafs and he disliked it, then said: People have permitted it, though they did not do it."
"14093","Ma'mar reported from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyab, who said about the sale of mushafs: Buy it, but do not sell it; have it written, but do not have it written for a wage."
"14094","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu al-Rabab al-Qushayri, who said: I was among the horsemen who conquered Tustar, and I was in charge of collecting with a group with me, when a man came to us with a chest and said: Will you sell me what is in this? We said: Yes, unless it be gold, silver, or the Book of Allah. He said: It is part of what you mention; in it is a book from the books of Allah. He said: So they opened the chest and in it was the Book of Daniel, so they gave it to the man as a gift and sold the chest for two dirhams. He said: And they mentioned that that man accepted Islam when he read the book."
"14095","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani, from Abu al-Duha, who said: A man came with mushafs selling them, so I asked Shurayh, Masruq, and 'Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Khatmi, and they said: We do not see taking a price for the Book of Allah the Exalted."
"14096","Ibn 'Uyaynah reported, saying: Abu Husayn reported from Abu al-Duha, who said: A man came with mushafs selling them, so I asked three without fail: Masruq, Shurayh, and 'Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Khatmi, and all of them disliked it and said: We do not see taking a price for the Book of Allah the Exalted."
"14097","'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman reported from 'Ata, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said about the sale of mushafs: Buy them but do not sell them. He said: And Ibn Jurayj said likewise, from 'Ata, that he heard Ibn 'Abbas saying it. 'Abd al-Quddus reported from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, the like of it."
"14098","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from 'Alqamah, who said: He was asked: Shall I buy a mushaf? He said: No."
"14099","Ma'mar reported from Layth, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, who said: I wish hands were cut off among those I saw selling mushafs."
"14100","Isra'il reported from Salim al-Aftas, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, who said: I heard Ibn 'Umar wishing: I wish I had seen hands cut off among those who buy mushafs."
"14101","Ma'mar reported from Matar al-Warraq, who said: Two scholars permitted the sale of mushafs: al-Hasan and al-Sha'bi."
"14102","From al-Thawri, from Dawud, from al-Sha'bi, who said: Only its paper and its workmanship are bought. And Khalid said it from al-Hasan."
"14103","Ja'far ibn Sulayman reported from Malik ibn Dinar, who said: Jabir ibn Zayd came to me while I was writing a mushaf, and said: An excellent deed is your deed; this is lawful earning, you transfer the Book of Allah from page to page. Malik said: And I asked al-Hasan and al-Sha'bi about it, and they saw no harm in it."
"14104","Isra'il informed, from Jabir, who said: I heard Salim ibn 'Abdullah, as he passed by those selling mushafs, say: An evil trade is this. A man said: What do you say, may Allah keep you right? He said: I heard Ibn 'Umar saying it."
"14105","Al-Thawri reported from Ibn Abi Layla, from his brother 'Isa, that 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla had a Christian from the people of al-Hirah write a mushaf for him for seventy dirhams."
"14106","Al-Thawri said, and al-A'mash informed me, from Ibrahim, that he disliked writing them for a wage."
"14107","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he was asked about a teacher taking a wage, and he said: If it is not taken with a stipulation, there is no harm in it. Ma'mar said: And Qatadah said the like of that."
"14108","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: They used to dislike taking a wage for teaching boys."
"14109","From al-Thawri, from Sa'id al-Jariri, from 'Abdullah ibn Shaqiq al-'Uqayli, who said: The Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were strict about the sale of mushafs, and disliked payment for boys in teaching."
"14110","Ma'mar reported from Qatadah, who said: People have innovated three things for which no wage used to be taken: the covering of the stallion, the division of wealth, and the teaching of boys."
"14111","'Uthman ibn Matar reported from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyab, al-Hasan, and Ibn Sirin: They disliked the reckoning of the dividers for a wage."
"14112","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Musa ibn Tarif, from his father, who said: 'Ali passed by a man reckoning between people for a wage, and 'Ali said to him: You are only consuming ill-gotten gain."
"14113","Al-Thawri said: And Abu Husayn informed me, from al-Qasim ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab disliked it."
"14114","Ibn 'Uyaynah reported from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Rafi', from Musa ibn Tarif, from his father, who said: 'Ali passed by a man dividing a division among people, and they said: O Commander of the Believers, give him his wage. He said: If he wishes, though it is ill-gotten gain."
"14115","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Qasim, from Ibrahim, that he disliked the wage of the wailing woman and the singing woman."
"14116","Ma'mar and Malik reported from al-Zuhri, who said: Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan informed us, saying: I exchanged silver for gold with Talhah ibn 'Ubaydullah, and he said: Wait for us until our treasurer comes to us from al-Ghabah. 'Umar heard them and said: No, by Allah, do not part from him until you take full payment from him, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Gold for silver is usury except hand to hand, wheat for wheat is usury except hand to hand, barley for barley is usury except hand to hand, and dates for dates is usury except hand to hand."
"14117","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn 'Umar, who said: 'Umar said: If one of you exchanges with his companion, let him not part from him until he takes it, and if he asks him to wait until he enters his house, let him not grant him respite, for I fear usury for you."
"14118","Ma'mar reported from Qatadah, who said: If you exchange a dinar for silver when the rate is thirteen and a half, and he is given fourteen and says: I will bring you half a dirham, there is no harm in this; he says: He takes the half dirham from him whenever he wishes. He said: But if the rate were thirteen and a half and he gave him thirteen and said: I shall bring you the half, then this is not proper."
"14119","Al-Thawri said: If you exchange for a dinar ten and a half dirhams, do not take for the half food or anything except silver; but if you stipulated ten dirhams and two mudds, there is no harm in it."
"14120","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Hisham ibn 'Amir, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Silver for gold is usury, except hand to hand."
"14121","Ma'mar and Ibn 'Uyaynah reported from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Salih, who said: Abu Sa'id al-Khudri met Ibn 'Abbas and said: What you give fatwa about exchange, is it something you found in the Book of Allah, or a sunnah from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: In neither of the two, and you, the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), know the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) better than I; but Usamah ibn Zayd informed me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Usury is in deferment. Abu Sa'id said: But I heard him saying: Gold for gold like for like, and silver for silver like for like."
"14122","Ma'mar reported from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu al-Minhal, who said: A man sold gold for silver until the season, and it was said to him: This is a sale that is not lawful. He said: I sold it in the market of the Muslims. Then Zayd ibn Arqam and al-Bara ibn 'Azib were mentioned to him, so he asked them both, and they said: No; we asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about exchange, and we were two traders, and he said: If it is hand to hand there is no harm, but no deferment."
"14123","Al-Thawri reported from Abu Hashim al-Wasiti, from Ziyad, who said: I was with Ibn 'Abbas at al-Ta'if, and he retracted his view on exchange seventy days before he died."
"14124","Ibn 'Uyaynah reported from Furat al-Qazzaz, who said: We visited Sa'id ibn Jubayr when he was ill, and 'Abd al-Malik al-Zarrad said to him: Ibn 'Abbas had withdrawn from his view on exchange. Sa'id said: My last knowledge of him, thirty-six nights before he died, was that he was still saying it. He said: And he contracted six and thirty with his hand."
"14125","Isra'il reported from Simak, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Umar, that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: May I buy gold with silver? He said: When you take one of the two, let your companion not part from you until there is no ambiguity between you and him."
"14126","Ibn 'Uyaynah reported from 'Amr ibn Dinar, who said: I heard Ibn 'Umar saying: If he asks you to wait the time of milking a she-camel, do not grant him respite."
"14127","From al-Thawri, from Ash'ath, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: Do not sell silver with a condition."
"14128","Al-Thawri said: If he says, whatever counterfeit comes to me that is not good I shall return to you, there is no harm in it; this is his right even if he did not stipulate, for stipulation is only saying: If I am pleased with it, otherwise I shall return it."
"14129","Ma'mar reported from Qatadah: He used to dislike saying in exchange, the weighing of the two is upon you. He said: And 'Ikrimah said the like of that, saying: That is deferment entering into exchange."
"14130","Ma'mar reported from Qatadah, who said: If there is counterfeit in it, there is no harm in exchanging it. And al-Hasan said it."
"14131","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, about a man to whom one hundred full-weight dinars were owed, then he lent him one hundred deficient dinars. He said: There is no harm in lending deficient dinars if those lent are full weight; but if you were lending him deficient ones and he lent you full weight, that would be disliked."
"14132","Al-Thawri said, about a man to whom another owes one hundred full-weight dinars, and he says: Lend me one hundred deficient dinars. He said: Take them from the one hundred full-weight ones, and I shall settle the excess with you and take it from you. He said: There is no harm in it."
"14133","Ma'mar reported, saying: Ibn Sirin was asked about one hundred mithqals of gold for one hundred mithqals of gold, in one of which is a mithqal of silver completing the hundred mithqals at that time, and he disliked it."
"14134","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, that he disliked the Syrian dinar for the Kufan dinar with an excess between them, taking the excess of the Syrian in silver."
"14135","From al-Thawri, from 'Uthman ibn al-Aswad, from Mujahid, about a man selling silver for silver with an excess between them. He said: He takes gold for its excess."
"14136","From al-Thawri, from 'Abd al-Wahid, from al-Hakam, that he saw no harm in taking the excess in silver."
"14137","'Abdullah ibn 'Umar reported from Nafi', who said: 'Umar said: Do not sell gold for gold, nor silver for silver, except like for like; do not prefer part of it over part, and do not sell absent of it for present. If he asks you to wait while he enters his house, do not grant him respite, for I fear usury for you both."
"14138","'Abdullah ibn 'Umar reported from Nafi', who said: It reached Ibn 'Umar that Abu Sa'id al-Khudri said about exchange, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Nafi' said: So Ibn 'Umar went, I with him, and Abu Sa'id said: My these two ears heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and my these two eyes saw him, saying: Do not sell gold for gold except like for like; do not favor part of it over part, and do not sell absent of it for present. Whoever increases and seeks increase has dealt in usury."
"14139","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Nafi', who said: A man came to Ibn 'Umar and said: Abu Sa'id gave me fatwa that gold for gold and silver for silver admit no increase between them. Nafi' said: So 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar took the man's hand, I with them, until we entered upon Abu Sa'id. Ibn 'Umar said: This one claims you narrated to him a hadith from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about exchange. He said: Yes; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with these my two ears, and saw with these my two eyes, that he said: Gold for gold, silver for silver, and do not favor part of it over part, and do not sell absent of it for present. Whoever increases and seeks increase has dealt in usury."
"14140","Al-Thawri reported, about a man who bought eight dirhams for a dinar and found four of them counterfeit. He said: If he finds them after parting from his companion, he returns them to him, and there is no reversal of sale between them; he shall have half a dinar, unless they begin a new sale for the half dinar, and the first four pass for half the dinar."
"14141","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Usury is only upon one who intends to deal in usury or to defer."
"14142","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab forbade silver for silver except like for like, and 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf or al-Zubayr said to him: Counterfeit coins are passed to us, so we give the bad and take the good. He said: Do not do it; rather go to al-Baqi', sell your garment for silver or goods, and when you have taken it and it is yours to sell, then discount what you wish and take silver if you wish."
"14143","Ma'mar reported from Abu Ishaq, from 'Abdullah ibn Kinanah, that Ibn Mas'ud exchanged silver for coins in the treasury, and when he came to Medina he asked, and it was said: It is not proper except like for like."
"14144","Abu Ishaq said: So Abu 'Amr al-Shaybani informed me that he saw Ibn Mas'ud going about returning them, passing by the money-changers and saying: Silver for silver is not proper except like for like."
"14145","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Rafi', who said: I went out and Abu Bakr al-Siddiq met me with two anklets, so I bought them from him; I placed them in one pan of the scale and placed my silver in the other pan, and it outweighed. I said: I make it lawful for you. He said: Even if you make it lawful for me, Allah has not made it lawful for me; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Silver for silver weight for weight, gold for gold weight for weight; the one who gives excess and the one who seeks it are in the Fire."
"14146","Al-Thawri reported from 'Abbas al-'Amiri, from Muslim ibn Nudhayr al-Sa'di, who said: I heard 'Ali, when a man asked him about a dirham for two dirhams. He said: That is prompt usury."
"14147","Ma'mar reported from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from 'Ali, that he was asked about a dirham for two dirhams, and he said: That is prompt usury."
"14148","Al-Thawri reported from Hammad, from a man, from Shurayh, who said 'Umar said: A dirham for a dirham, the excess between them is usury."
"14149","Ma'mar reported from 'Abd al-Karim, who said: A man sent silver with me to Mecca to buy goods for him, and there passed from me for his goods less than the silver he sent with me, so I asked Sa'id ibn Jubayr: Shall I take the dirhams he sent with me for himself, though there passed from me in their reckoning less than them? He said: No; discharge what he sent with you."
"14150","Malik said: Humayd ibn Qays informed me, from Mujahid, that a goldsmith asked Ibn 'Umar, saying: O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, I fashion, then sell the thing for more than its weight, taking as excess the measure of my work, or he said my wage. So he forbade him that, and the goldsmith kept repeating the question while Ibn 'Umar refused, until he reached his door, or he said the door of the mosque. Ibn 'Umar said: A dinar for a dinar, a dirham for a dirham, no excess between them; this is our Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) covenant to us, and our covenant to you."
"14151","Al-Taymi reported, from one who heard Yahya al-Bakka relating, from Abu Rafi', who said: I said to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab: O Commander of the Believers, I fashion gold and sell it for gold by its weight, taking a wage for its workmanship. He said: Do not sell gold for gold except weight for weight, silver for silver except weight for weight, and do not take excess."
"14152","Isra'il reported from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Rafi', from al-Qasim ibn Abi Bazzah, from Ya'qub, and Ibn 'Umar had bought from him until ease, then came to him paying coins better than his coins, and Ya'qub said: These are better than my coins. Ibn 'Umar said: It is a favor from before me; will you accept it? I said: Yes."
"14153","From al-Thawri, from Dawud, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Umar, that he saw no harm in taking dirhams for dinars and dinars for dirhams. Dawud said: And Sa'id ibn Jubayr used to give fatwa by it."
"14154","Al-Thawri said, about a man to whom a man lent a dinar, then he took from him dirhams at the rate of that day."
"14155","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Nafi', that Ibn 'Umar said: A man should not take dinars for dirhams, nor dirhams for dinars."
"14156","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: There is no harm in taking gold for silver and silver for gold."
"14157","Ma'mar reported from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, that he disliked dinars for dirhams and dirhams for dinars."
"14158","Abu Salamah said: Then Ibn 'Umar narrated to me that 'Umar said: If one of you sells gold for silver, let him not part from his companion, even if he goes behind the wall."
"14159","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: Ibn Mas'ud ordered a man to lend gold to his brother's sons, then he collected silver from them, so Ibn Mas'ud ordered him to return it and take gold from them."
"14160","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that the wife of Ibn Mas'ud sold a slavegirl of hers for gold and took silver, or sold for silver and took gold, then she asked 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he said: Take only that for which you sold."
"14161","Al-Thawri reported from al-Suddi, from 'Abdullah al-Bahi, from Yasar ibn Numayr, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab said about a man asking a man for dinars: Shall he take dirhams? He said: If it stands upon the price, give it to him by value."
"14162","Al-Thawri said: And al-Shaybani informed, from Musayyab ibn Rafi', that the wife of Ibn Mas'ud sold a slavegirl of hers for dirhams, and 'Abdullah ordered her to take dinars by value."
"14163","Al-Thawri reported from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, that he disliked selling gold for silver, then taking dirhams, saying: If I find a defect in it."
"14164","Al-Thawri said: And as for Mansur, he informed me, from al-Hakam, who said: Ibrahim ordered me to give his wife part of her dower in dinars from dirhams. 'Abd al-Razzaq said: Strange regarding the people of Basra and Kufa: the people of Kufa report license from 'Umar and 'Abdullah, while the people of Basra report strictness from them."
"14165","Al-Thawri said: And Yunus informed me, from al-Hasan, who said: There is no harm in it at the market rate. Sufyan said: There is no harm in it if both are pleased."
"14166","Sufyan said: And Layth informed me, from Tawus, that he disliked it in sale, but saw no harm in it in a loan."
"14167","I asked al-Thawri about a man to whom I had lent a dinar, then I took from him half a dinar. Jabir said: There remains for you upon him only half a dinar in gold. And he said about a man selling food for half a dinar to a term. Jabir said: It is only half a dinar in gold."
"14168","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that he disliked buying for a dinar except a dirham on credit, and saw no harm in it for cash."
"14169","Al-Thawri reported from Khalid ibn Dinar, from al-Harith ibn Yazid, from Ibrahim, that he used to dislike sale for a dinar except a dirham."
"14170","I asked Ma'mar about a man who sold a garment for a dinar less a dirham to a term, and he said: It is disliked. I said: Then he sold it for a dinar less a dirham. He said: Disliked. He said: Ibn Sirin used to dislike all this."
"14171","'Umar ibn Habib reported that one who heard Mujahid informed him, when asked about a man buying a garment for a dinar less a dirham to a term, that he disliked it, and disliked it if the dirham alone were on credit."
"14172","Ma'mar reported from 'Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, or Layth, or both, who said: A flogged man passed by Ibn al-Musayyab, and he said: What is his affair? They said: He used to clip dirhams. Ibn al-Musayyab said: It is corruption on earth."
"14173","Ibn 'Uyaynah reported from Yahya ibn Sa'id, who said: I heard Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab saying: Clipping gold and silver is part of corruption on earth."
"14174","Yahya ibn Rabi'ah reported, saying: I heard 'Ata ibn Abi Rabah, when asked about His Exalted saying: And there were in the city nine persons who caused corruption on earth and did not reform, say: They used to clip dirhams."
"14175","From Dawud ibn Qays, from Khalid ibn Rabi'ah ibn Hilal, from his father, who said: Ibn al-Zubayr came to Mecca and cut off the hand of a man who used to clip dirhams."
"14176","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn 'Umar, who said: I saw people in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) being beaten when a man bought food in bulk, lest he sell it in bulk until he conveyed it to his dwelling."
"14177","Ma'mar reported from Ibn al-Musayyab, who said of the established sunnah: If a man buys food or fat by measure, he should measure it before selling it; and when he sells it, it is also measured from him if he sells it by measure. He said: And it is not proper, when he has measured part of it, to buy its remainder in bulk, nor to sell it in bulk after buying it by measure."
"14178","Ibrahim ibn 'Umar reported from 'Abd al-Karim, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, that 'Uthman and his companions used to take a wasq of dates from Banu Qaynuqa', and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: How do you sell it? They said: With the profit of a sa' and two sa's. He said: Not until it is measured against you."
"14179","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: If you know the measure of the food, do not sell it in bulk to one who does not know what it is until he knows it."
"14180","Ibn al-Mubarak reported from al-Awza'i, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a man to sell food in bulk whose measure he knows until his companion knows it."
"14181","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, about a man who buys by measure, measures part of it, then says: Sell me the rest of it in bulk. He said: No, unless he rescinds the sale with him; if he rescinds it, let him buy it in bulk."
"14182","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim and Sulayman al-Taymi, about a man who measures a known measure in his vessels, then says to the buyer: I have measured in it such-and-such, but I sell you only in bulk. Both saw no harm in it. Sufyan said: This is among the best sales with us. Al-Thawri said: And Sulayman al-Taymi informed us that Ibn Sirin disliked it."
"14183","Al-Thawri said, about a man who bought food while a man looks at it: May he sell it to him in bulk without measuring it? He said: There is no harm in it."
"14184","Ma'mar reported from Qatadah, who said: I heard him saying: If you name a measure, then measure."
"14185","Ma'mar reported from Qatadah, who said: I heard him saying: I saw men seeing no harm in a man selling dates in bulk when he says: I have measured in it such-and-such. Ma'mar said: And Dawud ibn Abi Hind said that to me."
"14186","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: He was asked about a man who bought ghee or other in a container, weighed, and said: The container is such-and-such ratls. He disliked it and said: He deducts from the dirhams what he wishes in place of the container."
"14187","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, who said: 'Uthman used to buy camels with their loads, then say: Who will place a dinar in my hand? Who will give me profit for their halters?"
"14188","Ma'mar reported from one who heard 'Ikrimah saying: If you buy food and find it in excess, the excess belongs to the owner of the food and the deficiency is upon you."
"14189","From al-Thawri, from Ash'ath, from al-Sha'bi and al-Hakam, about food you bought and found in excess. Both said: Return the excess to its owner, and the deficiency is upon the buyer."
"14190","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: If the two sa's differ, what increases is yours and what decreases is upon you."
"14191","Ma'mar reported from al-Hasan and al-Zuhri, both saying: If a man frees his slave, the wealth belongs to the slave."
"14192","Al-Thawri reported from Mughirah, Ibrahim, al-Shaybani, and Isma'il, from al-Sha'bi, both saying: If a man sells the slave who has wealth, the wealth follows the slave."
"14193","Al-Thawri reported from Layth, from Tawus, who said: If the slave is freed or contracts kitabah, the wealth belongs to the slave."
"14194","Waki' reported from Shu'bah, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: If he frees him, the wealth belongs to the slave; if he sells him, the wealth belongs to the buyer."
"14195","Ma'mar reported from Qatadah, who said: If he frees him or sells him, the wealth belongs to the master."
"14196","From al-Thawri, from Abu Khalid, from 'Imran ibn 'Umayr, from his father, who was a slave boy of 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, whom he freed, then said: Your wealth is only my wealth. Then he said: It is yours."
"14197","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Anas ibn Sirin, that Anas ibn Malik asked a slave of his about his wealth, and he informed him of much wealth, so he freed him and said: Your wealth is yours."
"14198","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn 'Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever sells a slave, his wealth belongs to the seller unless the buyer stipulates; and whoever sells palm trees in which the fruit has been pollinated, its fruit belongs to the seller unless the buyer stipulates. Ma'mar reported from Matar al-Warraq, from 'Ikrimah ibn Khalid, from Ibn 'Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, who said: Nafi' said: It is only from 'Umar about the affair of the slave."
"14199","'Abdullah ibn 'Umar reported from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, who said: 'Umar said: Whoever sells a slave having wealth, his wealth belongs to the seller unless the buyer stipulates."
"14200","Isra'il reported from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Rafi', from Ibn Abi Mulaykah and 'Ata ibn Abi Rabah, both saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever sells pollinated palms, its fruit belongs to the seller unless the buyer stipulates."
"14201","Al-Thawri reported, saying: If a man sells land and stipulates its fruit, and the buyer says: Take your crop from the land, while the seller says: Its food has not been harvested. He said: He harvests it if it has not been harvested, because he says: Vacate my land; but if the seller stipulates upon him that the food stays in his land for two months, he bears liability for the land if a calamity strikes it."
"14202","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, and from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyab, all saying: There is no harm in saying: I sell you this garment for ten until a month, or for twenty until two months, then he sells it upon one of the two before parting from him, there is no harm in it. From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Tawus, the like of it."
"14203","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that he used to dislike saying: I sell you this for such-and-such until a month, or until two months."
"14204","Ma'mar and al-Thawri reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, who said: Whoever makes two sales in one sale, he gets the lesser of the two, or usury."
"14205","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that he used to dislike saying: I sell you for ten dinars cash, or for fifteen to a term. Ma'mar said: And al-Zuhri and Qatadah saw no harm in that if he parted from him upon one of the two."
"14206","Ma'mar and Ibn 'Uyaynah reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: If he says: It is for such-and-such until such-and-such, and for such-and-such until such-and-such, and the sale falls upon this, then it is at the lesser of the two prices to the farther of the two terms. Ma'mar said: And this is when the buyer has consumed it."
"14207","Al-Thawri said: If you say: I sell you for cash until such, and on credit for such-and-such, and the buyer takes it away, then he has the choice in the two sales so long as no sale has fallen upon one of them; but if the sale falls thus, this is disliked, it is two sales in one sale, it is rejected, and it is what is forbidden. If you find your goods intact you take them, and if they have been consumed, you get the lesser of the two prices and the farther of the two terms."
"14208","Isra'il reported, saying: Simak ibn Harb narrated, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abdullah, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: Two bargains in one bargain are not proper: that he says: It is for such-and-such on credit, and for such-and-such for cash."
"14209","Ma'mar reported, saying: It reached me that Ibn 'Umar used to buy until ease without naming a term."
"14210","Isra'il reported from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Rafi', from al-Qasim ibn Abi Bazzah, from Ya'qub, that Ibn 'Umar used to buy from him until ease without naming a term."
"14211","Al-Thawri and Isra'il reported from Simak ibn Harb, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: Two bargains in one bargain is riba. Sufyan said: That means he sold him a sale and said: I sell you this for ten dinars, for which you give me the exchange of your dirhams."
"14212","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, from Masruq, concerning a man who says: I sell you this cloth for such-and-such dinars, you giving me the dinar at ten dirhams. Masruq said: Abd Allah said: Two bargains in one bargain are not lawful."
"14213","Al-Thawri reported concerning a man who bought goods from a man for such-and-such, and he gifted him the price. He said: No, until he names the gift."
"14214","Al-Thawri said concerning a man who advanced a man one hundred dinars for something, then when he went to weigh out the dinars for him, he said: Give me dirhams or goods for them. He said: It is disliked, because it is two sales in one sale."
"14215","Al-Thawri said concerning a man who sold goods to a man for a dinar, then came to him afterwards and said: Give me dirhams for the dinar, so he gave him dirhams, then he learned that the goods were stolen, so they were returned. He said: He returns the dirhams to him, because the sale was void, for it was currency exchange. But if he took goods, he returns a dinar to him, because that is not like exchange. And if he bought a slave-girl and found a defect in her, while he had taken dirhams for the dinars, then he returns the dinars."
"14216","He said: Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, and Ayyub from Ibn Sirin, who both said: When you advance food to a man here, and he gives it to you in another land, then if it was stipulated, it is disliked, but if it is by way of kindness, there is no harm."
"14217","Al-Thawri reported from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: Ibn al-Zubayr used to borrow funds from the merchants, then write orders for them to his officials. He said: So I mentioned that to Ibn Abbas, and he said: There is no harm in it. Al-Thawri said: And Ibrahim used to dislike it."
"14218","Ibn Uyaynah reported from Abu Umays, from Ibn Abbas, who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, had given Zaynab, the wife of Ibn Mas'ud, dates or barley at Khaybar. Asim ibn Adi said to her: Would you like me to give you its equivalent at Madina, and take this for my slaves here? She said: Until I ask Umar. So she asked him, and he said: How about the liability? As if he disliked it."
"14219","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, who said: My father used to advance food to people from his land, which was nearer than al-Janad than their land, and he said: Carry it to al-Janad, and he gave them the hire for what is between his land and al-Janad."
"14220","Al-Thawri said concerning a man who advanced a man five hundred faraq, to give it to him in a specified land, then he met him in another land, and said: Measure out your food from me here, and I will carry it for you on my animals to the land I stipulated for you. He said: It is disliked for him to carry it, because he took food and took the hire as surplus."
"14221","Al-Thawri said concerning a man who was owed food by a man at Judda, so he carried it to Mecca, then said: Give me its hire for which I carried it from Judda. He said: He has no hire, if he willingly chooses to deliver it to him in full at Mecca."
"14222","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: Ubayy ibn Ka'b borrowed wealth from Umar ibn al-Khattab, I think he said ten thousand, then Ubayy gifted him afterwards from his dates, which ripened early, and they were among the finest dates of the people of Madina. Umar returned them to him, so Ubayy said: I am sending your wealth; I have no need of anything that deprives me of my fine dates. So he accepted them, and said: Riba is only upon him who intends to profit and to defer."
"14223","From al-Thawri, from Yunus ibn Ubayd and Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Ibn Sirin, that Ubayy ibn Ka'b borrowed ten thousand from Umar, so Ubayy sent him from his dates, which were among the finest dates of the people of Madina, and his dates ripened early. Umar returned them to him, so Ubayy said: I have no need of anything that deprives me of my dates. So Umar accepted them, and said: Riba is only upon him who intends to profit and to defer."
"14224","From al-Thawri, from Mansur and al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, who said: When you stay as a guest with a man who owes you a debt, and you eat at his expense, then deduct for him what you ate at his place, except that Ibrahim used to say: Unless it was customary kindness which they used to extend to each other before that."
"14225","Ma'mar reported from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, who said: When you lend a man a loan, do not accept from him the gift of a trotter, nor the loan of riding an animal."
"14226","From al-Thawri, from Ammar al-Duhni, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, who said: A man came to Ibn Abbas and said: He was the neighbor of Simak, so I lent him fifty dirhams, and he used to send me from his fish. Ibn Abbas said: Settle the account with him; if there is surplus, return it to him, and if it is equal, set it off."
"14227","From al-Thawri, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Kulthum ibn al-Aqmar, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: I came to Ubayy ibn Ka'b and said: I intend to go to Iraq for jihad, so lower your wing to me. Ubayy ibn Ka'b said to me: You are going to a land in which riba is widespread. So when you lend a man a loan and he gives you a gift, then take your loan and return his gift to him."
"14228","Ma'mar reported from Sa'id ibn Abi Burdah, from Abu Burdah, who said: My father sent me to Abd Allah ibn Salam to learn from him. So I came to him and he asked me: Who are you? So I told him, and he welcomed me. I said: My father sent me to you to ask you and learn from you. He said: O son of my brother, you are in a land of merchants. So when you have wealth owed by a man, and he gifts you a rope of straw, do not accept it, for it is riba."
"14229","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, who said: A man came to Ibn Umar and said: I lent a man a loan, and he gave me a gift. He said: Return his gift to him, or recompense him."
"14230","Isra'il reported from Abu Ishaq, from a man, from Ibn Umar, that a man said to him: I lent a man a loan, and he gave me a gift. He said: Recompense him in place of his gift, or count it for him against what he owes, or return it to him."
"14231","Ma'mar reported from Isma'il ibn Shurus, from al-Hasan ibn Muslim ibn Yannaq, who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, borrowed barley from a man, then repaid him and added more. That was mentioned to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he said: It is a gain for you."
"14232","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: Every loan that draws benefit is disliked. Ma'mar said: And Qatadah said it too."
"14233","Ma'mar and Ibn Uyaynah reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: A man borrowed five hundred dinars from a man on condition that he lend him the back of his horse. Ibn Mas'ud said: Whatever benefit you obtain from the back of his horse is riba."
"14234","Al-Thawri reported from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: Every loan that draws benefit, there is no good in it."
"14235","Ma'mar reported from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib and al-Hasan, who both said: There is no harm for a man to lend white dirhams and take black, or lend black and take white, so long as there is no stipulation between them."
"14236","Isra'il reported: Isa ibn Abi Azzah told me, he said: I borrowed a deficient dinar from a man, and I had nothing except a dinar exceeding his dinar. So I said to him: It is yours. Al-Sha'bi said: What is that? So I told him, and he said: It is not lawful for him. I said: I make it lawful for him. He said: Even if you make it lawful for him, it has become lawful."
"14237","From Malik, that it reached him that a man came to Ibn Umar and said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, I advanced a loan to a man, and I also stipulated upon him better than what I lent him. Ibn Umar said: That is riba. He said: Then what do you advise me? He said: A loan is of three kinds: a loan by which you seek the Face of Allah, so yours is the Face of Allah; a loan by which you seek the face of its owner, so yours is only his face; and a loan which you lent to take thereby bad for good. He said: Then what do you advise me? He said: I think you should tear up your deed; if he gives you the like of what you lent him, accept it; if he gives you less than what you lent him and you take it, you are rewarded; and if he gives you better than what you lent him, with his soul content with it, then that is gratitude he shows you, and it is the reward for your respite."
"14238","Abd Allah ibn Kathir reported from Shu'bah, who said: I asked al-Hakam and Hammad about a man who hands a man dirhams, and he returns to him better than them. They both said: If it was not his intention, there is no harm."
"14239","From Asim, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: Ibn Mas'ud said: Suht is a bribe in religion. Sufyan said: Meaning in judgment."
"14240","From al-Thawri, from Aban, from Abu Nadrah, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Gifts to rulers are misappropriation."
"14241","Ma'mar and al-Thawri reported from Mansur, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Masruq, who said: A man from among the people of our land came and sought Masruq's help against an injustice he suffered at the hands of Ibn Ziyad, so he helped him. He then brought him a slave-girl of his after that, so he returned her to him, and said: I heard Abd Allah say: This is suht."
"14242","From al-Thawri, from Kulayb ibn Wa'il, who said: I asked Ibn Umar, I said: A great dihqan of heavy kharaj came to me, so I guaranteed his kharaj on his behalf. He then came to me, tore up his deed, paid what he owed, then mounted me on a mule and clothed me with a robe. He said: Tell me, had you not guaranteed for him, would he have given you this? I said: No. He said: Then no."
"14243","From al-Thawri, from Abu Husayn, from Shurayh, who said: May Allah curse the briber and the bribed."
"14244","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman, or he said: from his uncle al-Harith, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The curse of Allah is upon the briber and the bribed."
"14245","Isma'il ibn Abd Allah reported: Ibrahim ibn Uthman, a man from the descendants of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, told me, he said: I was with Umar ibn Abi Salamah at Abd al-Aziz ibn Marwan's, and it was as if he was slow in admitting him. So I mentioned that to him, and he said: I found nothing amiss in my companion, but the doorkeeper asked me for something. I said: Then give him. He said: I have nothing with me to give him, but it reached me that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: May Allah curse the briber and the bribed, so I dislike giving him anything for that."
"14246","Ma'mar reported from one who heard al-Hasan, who said: Whatever you give from your wealth to conciliate for your wealth and your blood, you are rewarded therein. And al-Thawri said it from Ibrahim."
"14247","Ibn Uyaynah reported from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir ibn Zayd Abu al-Sha'tha, who said: I heard him say: Nothing was more beneficial to the people than bribery in the time of Ziyad, or he said: Ibn Ziyad."
"14248","Abu Sufyan reported from Mu'adh ibn al-Ala, from his father, who said: Ali addressed us at Kufa with a flask in his hand, wearing trousers and sandals, and said: I have acquired nothing since I entered it except this flask, which a dihqan gifted me."
"14249","Al-Thawri reported from Mansur, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, who said: Aishah said: When Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down the verses, the verses of riba from the end of Surah al-Baqarah, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stood and recited them to us, and he forbade trade in wine."
"14250","From al-Thawri, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Dharr ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: A man came to him and said: I have a neighbor who consumes riba, and he keeps inviting me. He said: Its enjoyment is for you, and its sin is upon him. Sufyan said: But if you recognize it specifically, do not take it. Ma'mar reported from Mansur, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Dharr, from Ibn Mas'ud the like of it."
"14251","Ma'mar reported from Abu Ishaq, from al-Zubayr ibn Adi, from Salman al-Farisi, who said: If you have a friend who is an official, or a neighboring official, or a relative who is an official, and he gives you a gift or invites you to food, accept it, for its enjoyment is for you and its sin is upon him."
"14252","Ma'mar reported: Adi ibn Artah used to send to al-Hasan every day bowls of tharid, and he would eat from them and feed his companions."
"14253","Ma'mar reported from Mansur, who said: I said to Ibrahim: An arif of ours comes down and takes from wrongdoing, then invites me, so I do not accept. He said: Satan presents this to cause enmity; the officials used to come down and take, then invite, and they were accepted."
"14254","Ma'mar reported from Mansur, who said: I said to Ibrahim: I stayed with an official, so he lodged me and gave me a gift. He said: Accept. I said: What about a man of riba? He said: Accept, so long as you do not command him or help him."
"14255","Ma'mar reported: Al-Hasan was asked: Is the food of the money-changers eaten? He said: Allah has kept you after the Jews and Christians; they consume riba, yet He made their food lawful for you."
"14256","Ma'mar reported: Adi ibn Artah sent wealth to al-Hasan, al-Sha'bi, and Muhammad ibn Sirin; al-Hasan and al-Sha'bi accepted, and Ibn Sirin refused."
"14257","From al-Thawri, from Isa ibn al-Mughirah, from al-Sha'bi, who said: Umar said: We left nine-tenths of the lawful out of fear of riba."
"14258","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that he used to say concerning a slave-girl whom a man buys and has intercourse with her, then finds a defect in her. He said: She is from the wealth of the buyer, and the seller returns the difference between soundness and defect."
"14259","From al-Thawri, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Ali ibn Husayn, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he used to say concerning a slave-girl with whom the buyer has intercourse, then finds a defect in her. He said: She is from the wealth of the buyer, and the seller returns the difference between soundness and defect."
"14260","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, who said: There is deducted from him according to the defect, and he is bound by the defect."
"14261","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: When he finds a defect in her after he has had intercourse with her, then if she was a virgin, he returns her and returns a tenth with her, and if she was non-virgin, then half a tenth."
"14262","Ma'mar reported from Qatadah, the like of Ibrahim's saying. He said: And Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz said: When he has intercourse with her while she has a defect, then he does not return her if he found the defect after he had intercourse with her."
"14263","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: I heard Shurayh being asked, while at Basra, about a man who bought a slave-girl and had intercourse with her, then found a defect in her. He said to the buyer: Would you like me to say that you committed zina? He said: Then he afterwards gave judgment, while at Kufa, for the dower."
"14264","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, who said: He returns her and returns the dower."
"14265","From al-Thawri, from Ash'ath, from al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh, who said: If she was a virgin, then a tenth, and if she was non-virgin, then half a tenth."
"14266","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, who said: If he wishes, he returns her, and returns a tenth of a dinar with her."
"14267","Ma'mar reported from Hammad, concerning a man who bought a garment with a tear in it, then cut it. He said: It is upheld against him, and the amount of the defect is deducted from him. Ma'mar said: And Qatadah said: It is allowed."
"14268","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: A man disputed with Shurayh about a garment he sold, in which its owner found a tear. He said: And he had already worn it. The buyer said: Uthman, Commander of the Believers, judged that whoever finds a flaw in a garment should return it. So Shurayh upheld it against him. When the man left him, he said: This judge of yours claims that the judgment of the Commander of the Believers is base and vile, and his own judgment is just. Shurayh met him and said: When you meet me, you meet in me an unjust imam, and when I meet you, I meet in you a wicked man; you displayed complaint and concealed the judgment."
"14269","Abd Allah ibn Kathir reported: I heard him narrate from Jabalah ibn Suhaym, who said: I saw Ibn Umar buy a shirt and wear it, then he wanted to return it, but some yellowness from his beard got on it, so he disliked returning it."
"14270","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, who said: Two men disputed before him about a man who bought an animal, then travelled on it, then returned and found a defect in it. The seller said: He has travelled on it. Shurayh said: You permitted him its back."
"14271","Isra'il reported from Majza'ah ibn Zahir: A woman disputed with him before Shurayh about an animal she bought, in which there were two swellings. Its owner said: This is only from the dusty ground. Its owner took it back, and six months passed, and she went out on it on a journey, then returned and showed it, and it was a bone abscess. She found two witnesses that this abscess was from the swellings, so Shurayh returned it against him."
"14272","From al-Thawri, from Ghaylan, from al-Hakam, who said: A man bought a mule and found a defect in it, while it had become emaciated. He returns it and returns the emaciation."
"14273","From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Shaybani and Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, concerning a man who bought slaves in a lot and found a defect in some of them. He said: He returns them all, or takes them all. Sufyan said: And we do not say that; we say: The buyer has the choice; what is found defective is valued, and he returns it specifically, and if he wishes, he returns them all."
"14274","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata: He returns the defective one, and the rest is binding upon him at its value."
"14275","Ma'mar reported from Hammad, concerning a man who bought slaves in a lot, and one of them had a defect. He said: He returns them all, or takes them all. He said: And I asked Ibn Shubrumah about it, and he said: The defect is valued, then he returns to the seller, because the specific slave may be among the slaves."
"14276","Al-Thawri said concerning a man who sold two garments for twenty, then the buyer sold one of them for thirty, and found a defect in the other. We valued the one he sold for thirty at twenty, and valued the other at fifteen, so it is over seven shares."
"14277","Al-Thawri reported from Hammad, from Ibrahim, concerning a man who buys a slave with a defect, then a defect occurs in him while with the buyer. He said: He returns the defect for its defect, and if a new occurrence happens to him, it is from the wealth of the buyer, and the seller returns the excess of what is between soundness and defect. He said: And al-Hakam said: Return it and return the occurrence."
"14278","Ma'mar reported from Qatadah, who said: When you sell a slave with a defect, then another defect occurs while with the buyer, it is binding upon the buyer. Ma'mar said: And Ibn Shubrumah said: He returns to the seller and gives him what occurred with him of defect."
"14279","Al-Thawri reported from Ash'ath, from Ali ibn Mudrik, who said: Two men disputed before al-Dahhak ibn Qays about goods in which dubaylah was found, which is an old disease known not to be newly occurring, so he judged it against the seller. Sufyan said: And Sulayman al-Shaybani told me, from al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh, that he used to say to him: People know that, meaning that it does not newly occur. He said: Bring me two men from among the people that he sold it to you with that disease. The saying of al-Dahhak is dearer to Sufyan: when it is known that it is not of what newly occurs, he returns it without proof, and the oath of the buyer is taken that he did not see it before buying it, did not accept it after seeing it, and did not offer it for sale after seeing the disease, when it is known that it does not newly occur."
"14280","Hisham ibn Hassan reported from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, who said: When he offers the goods for sale while knowing there is a defect in them, they become binding upon him. Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, the like of it."
"14281","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, that three men disputed before him about a slave-girl. One of them said: This one sold me a slave-girl with a disease. The other said: I bought from this one and sold to this one. Shurayh said: Yours is like what is upon you. Then he took his oath by Allah that he had sold her without knowing this disease-defect in her, and had not concealed a defect he knew. The man swore to that, then said: And I would never conceal a defect from a Muslim. Shurayh said: That is better for you. Then he returned her to the first, because the first had sold her with that disease, and he only made the middle one swear because he used to judge: whoever sees a disease, then offers for sale, it has become binding upon him."
"14282","Al-Thawri said concerning two disputing men: one of them sold goods, and both acknowledged the sale and the goods, even if there was no proof. The judge says to the buyer: Swear by Allah that you have not offered it for sale, nor accepted it since you saw it. And if there was proof, he is also made to swear, and the seller is made to swear that he did not sell it while it had it."
"14283","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, who said: I heard him say: Whoever stipulates that it has no defect, then he returns whenever he wishes for the slightest defect."
"14284","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, who said: The liability of a Muslim toward his brother stands even if he did not stipulate freedom from disease, deceit, blemish, or khabthah. And khabthah means theft."
"14285","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: Two men disputed before Shurayh. One of them said: This one sold me a slave-girl, then when the sale became binding, he said: There is a disease in her. Shurayh said: Take her; if you find in her what he said, then he has testified against himself."
"14286","From al-Thawri, from Awf, from Anas ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, who said: If a man sold goods, then when the sale became binding, he said: I declare myself free to you from such-and-such defect. He said: It does not free him; if he sees anything in her, he returns her, and he is held by his admission."
"14287","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, who said: Two men disputed before Iyas ibn Mu'awiyah. One of them said: This one declared me free from qarn. The other said: I did not know what qarn was. The other said: I thought it was a protruding horn on his head. So he suspected him, and he upheld the sale."
"14288","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: There was disputed before Shurayh about a man who sold a slave with a brand on his forehead at the hairline, so he dressed him with a cap and his owner did not know of it. Shurayh said: You concealed the disease and covered the blemish, so return him to him."
"14289","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: I never saw the judges allow exemption from disease except what you specified and placed your hand upon."
"14290","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: Nothing is allowed except what you named; but to name a disease while mixing another with it, then no. And Mughirah said: You are free from what you named."
"14291","Ma'mar reported from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, concerning a man who sells goods and declares himself free from disease. He said: He is free from what he named. It was said to Ibrahim: A man says: I sell you meat on a board, and I am free from whatever the earth carried away of it. He said: No, until he names; if he names, then he is free from what he named. Abd al-Razzaq said: And the people follow it."
"14292","Al-Thawri reported from Mansur, from one of his companions, from Shurayh, who said: He is not free until he places his hand upon the disease. And Ibn Jurayj said it from Ata."
"14293","Ma'mar reported from Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari, from Salim ibn Abd Allah ibn Umar, who said: Ibn Umar sold a slave of his with exemption, and the buyer found a defect in him. He said to Ibn Umar: You did not name it to me. They disputed before Uthman ibn Affan. The man said: He sold me a slave with a disease he did not name to me. Ibn Umar said: I sold with exemption. Uthman judged that Ibn Umar should swear by Allah that he sold him while there was no disease in him that he knew. Ibn Umar refused to swear, and took back the slave. Abd al-Razzaq said: As for the people of Madina, they judge by exemption; they say: when he declares himself free to him, he is free of it. And the people follow other than it, until he names that disease."
"14294","Malik and al-Aslami reported from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Salim: Ibn Umar sold a slave-boy of his, I think he said for seven hundred dirhams, and sold him with exemption. The buyer said to Ibn Umar: There is a disease in the slave you did not name to me. They disputed before Uthman. The man said: He sold me a slave with a disease he did not name to me. Ibn Umar said: I sold him with exemption. Uthman judged that Ibn Umar should swear by Allah that he sold him while there was no disease in him that he knew. He said: Ibn Umar refused to swear, and took back the slave."
"14295","From al-Thawri: Concerning liability after death, his heirs return in his place."
"14296","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, concerning liability after death. He said: There is reduced from him according to the defect."
"14297","Ma'mar reported from Qatadah, who said: There is no liability after death; when he dies, it is binding upon him."
"14298","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, who said: A man said to him: This one sold me a slave-girl with a disease. He said: Return her with her disease. He said: She has died. He said: Your proof that that disease is what killed her."
"14299","From al-Thawri, from Zakariyya, from al-Sha'bi, that a man bought a slave then freed him, and found a defect in him. He said: The excess of what is between them is returned against his owner, and what is returned against him is placed in freeing necks, because he had already directed him."
"14300","Sufyan said concerning a slave shared between two men, one of whom bought from his partner, then found a defect in him. He said: He has liability against his partner, meaning he returns him if he wishes."
"14301","Ibn al-Taymi reported: Ibn Shubrumah was asked about a man who sold goods for an absent man: is liability upon him? He said: Yes. It was said: What if he had informed them that they belonged to another? He said: Even so, unless he stipulates to them at the sale that your liability is upon the owner of the goods."
"14302","From al-Thawri, concerning a man who bought a slave from a man, then travelled with him to a distant land, then a stolen person recognized the slave in his hands. He said: I decree against him, and I decree what is his against the one from whom he buys."
"14303","From al-Thawri, concerning two men who exchanged slaves, then one of them found a defect in one of the two slaves: the value of the defective slave. He said: This is Ibn Abi Layla."
"14304","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: They return the defect between them; whichever of them took, returns to his partner what that defect reduced."
"14305","Al-Thawri reported from Abu Sahl, from al-Sha'bi, concerning a man who bought a slave who commits zina and drinks wine. He said: Shurayh returned for zina."
"14306","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, who said: There was disputed before him about a slave-girl who committed zina. He said: One returns for zina. The man said: She is non-Arab. Shurayh said: Whoever wishes returns for zina."
"14307","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, who said: One returns in sale for all suspicions: zina, theft, drinking wine, and the like."
"14308","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, and from al-Thawri, from Mutarrif, from al-Sha'bi, who both said: Pregnancy is uncertainty for which one returns in the slave-girl sold. And Ma'mar said it from Qatadah."
"14309","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-A'la, from Shurayh: That he used to return the slave sold for harshness."
"14310","Al-Thawri reported from Abu Husayn, from Amir: That he used to return for a nail defect, and for a disfiguring mole."
"14311","Al-Thawri reported from Abd al-A'la, from Shurayh, that he used to return for foolishness. A foolish slave-girl was disputed before him, so he said: Place for her a bowl of water; if she drinks then plunges into it, she is foolish, and if she lifts it to her, she is not foolish."
"14312","One of our companions reported from Hammad, concerning a man who bought a slave and was told that he fled while small. He said: One does not return for that; one only returns for that when he does it while grown."
"14313","Al-Thawri reported from Abu Ishaq, who said: Shurayh used not to return for stumbling, saying: All animals stumble. Sufyan said: It is a defect for which one returns."
"14314","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, who said: He used to return the mule when it was donkey-like, following donkeys and leaving horses, when its owner did not disclose that, and he counted it a defect."
"14315","From al-Thawri, from Hisham, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, concerning the donkey-like mule. He said: It is placed in a house with horses and donkeys, then observed which of them it follows."
"14316","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, who said: Two men disputed before Shurayh. One of them said: I bought from this one a sheep that eats flies. He said: Milk for free."
"14317","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: A man dyed a garment of his the color of Harawi, then went out to sell it. A man met him and said: For how much do you sell this Harawi? The man said: For such-and-such. He sold it to him, then examined it, and it was not Harawi. He disputed with him before Shurayh. Shurayh said: Did he stipulate to you that it is Harawi? He said: No. So he upheld it against him, and said: Had he been able to beautify his garment otherwise, he would have done so."
"14318","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: Shurayh used to make swear upon knowledge: I did not intend that against him."
"14319","Ma'mar reported from Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari, from Salim, that Uthman used to make swear upon knowledge."
"14320","From al-Thawri, from Mutarrif, from al-Sha'bi, who said: What is seen of disease, he is made to swear decisively; what is not seen, he is made to swear upon knowledge."
"14321","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, who said: He is made to swear decisively."
"14322","Ma'mar reported from Mughirah, from al-Sha'bi, who said: He is made to swear decisively. Al-Sha'bi's saying was mentioned to Ibn Sirin, who used to say like Shurayh's saying; when al-Sha'bi's saying was mentioned to him, he said: Then I do not know."
"14323","He said: And al-Sha'bi said: When a man claims a debt for his father, he swears decisively that his father collected nothing of it, unless the other swears decisively that he did collect."
"14324","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: When a man claims a debt for his father, he is made to swear upon knowledge."
"14325","From Umar ibn Dharr, who said: Al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman used to make swear by Allah that he does not ward off a right he knows for him."
"14326","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: Two men disputed before Shurayh. One of them said: I hired out my animal to this one, and the lion ate it. Shurayh said: He needs it more than you, and he did not make him liable for it. From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Shurayh the like of it; from al-Thawri, from Hisham, from Muhammad, from Shurayh the like of it."
"14327","Al-Thawri reported from al-Hajjaj, from Uthman, from Shurayh, who said: There is no liability upon the hirer."
"14328","Isra'il reported from Ash'ath, from Shurayh, who said: When the hirer violates, he is liable."
"14329","Ibn Jurayj reported: I said to Ata: I wrote against two men in a sale that the living of you is upon your dead, and your solvent upon your insolvent. He said: It is allowed. And Amr ibn Dinar and Sulayman ibn Musa said: Allowed. And Sulayman said: Shurayh said: Allowed."
"14330","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Jahdam, who said: I disputed before Shurayh, and I had written against people: whichever of them I wish, he settles my right. A man among them settled for me, and said: Upon me is only my share. Shurayh said to me: Take whichever of them you wish. So I took the easiest of them, and he was the easiest of them."
"14331","Ma'mar reported from Qatadah, who said: When you write that the living of you is upon your dead, and your solvent upon your insolvent, then it is allowed."
"14332","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub and others, from Ibn Sirin, who said: When he says: whichever of them I wish, I take my whole right or separately. He said: I like him to stipulate likewise."
"14333","From al-Thawri, who said: When he says: some of them are guarantors for others, and whichever of them I wish, I take my right, whether altogether or separately, he takes them altogether if he wishes, and separately if he wishes."
"14334","Ma'mar and al-Thawri reported from Ibn Shubrumah, who said: When he says: whichever of them I wish, I take my whole right, then he takes only by shares. Ma'mar said, and Ibn Shubrumah said: But if each of them is guarantor for his companion, then it is allowed."
"14335","Al-Thawri said: There is no liability upon a partner, when he guarantees for his partner against a debtor of theirs, because neither of them should collect to the exclusion of his partner."
"14336","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, that Ibn Shurayh guaranteed a man himself, so Shurayh imprisoned him in jail, and said: Send him food and drink."
"14337","Isma'il ibn Ayyash reported from Shurahbil ibn Muslim, from Abu Umamah, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: The guarantor is liable."
"14338","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: There was disputed before Shurayh about a man who said to a man: Pay to so-and-so fifty dirhams, and I am its guarantor. The man claimed he had paid them, so Shurayh said: Your proof of what you paid, otherwise his oath, by Allah, that he does not know he paid him anything. It was as if the man feared the oath, so Shurayh said: Then I swear by Allah: I do not know he paid him anything. His opponent said: You have allured him away from an oath he would not have undertaken."
"14339","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: I heard Shurayh say: Your proof that you demand him, then I affirm."
"14340","A companion of ours said: Ibn Abi Layla was asked about a man who said: Whatever you trade with this one for, I am its guarantor, and whatever is upon him, I am its surety. He said: It is nothing until he sets a term. He said: And Abu Hanifah said: That binds him. He said: And Ya'qub said it too."
"14341","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: I sold a mare of mine, and a slave-boy of Ibn Ziyad guaranteed for me. I disputed with him before Shurayh, and said: I was barred from my guarantor, my named wealth was collected, and my debtor's wealth was divided without me. Shurayh answered me and said: If he was free and guaranteed for you, he is liable; if your named wealth was collected, you are most entitled to it; and if his wealth was divided without you, it is by shares."
"14342","From Hisham ibn Hassan narrating, that Muhammad ibn Sirin told him, he said: I sold a mare of mine, and a slave-boy of Abd Allah ibn Ziyad guaranteed for me. I came demanding him, and we disputed before his master. We sat before him, and he raised his voice and changed. I said: Return me and him to the judge. So he sent with us a messenger to Shurayh, and said: Tell me what he judges between them. We went to Shurayh and sat before him. I said: I sold a mare of mine, and a slave-boy of Ibn Ziyad guaranteed for me, so I was barred from my debtor, my named wealth was collected, and my debtor's wealth was divided without me. Shurayh answered me and said: If he was free and guaranteed for you, he is liable; if your named wealth was collected, you are most entitled to it; and if the creditors took his wealth without you, it is among you by shares. The messenger returned to him and told him, and he said nothing."
"14343","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, concerning a slave's guarantee: It is nothing; it is not of trade."
"14344","Al-Thawri said: Whoever upholds this deed, then its contents are for me. A man stood, it is nothing until his authority is established."
"14345","Ma'mar reported from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh, who said: Two men disputed before him about a house one of them sold his companion, then the sale was annulled. The man said: Then where is the yield of my house? Shurayh said: Then where is the profit of his wealth?"
"14346","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani, from al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh, who said: If a man buys a slave-boy and profits from him, then finds a defect in him, what he used of him is for his liability."
"14347","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Makhlad ibn Khaffaf, who said: I bought a slave jointly between me and partners, then he was taken back from it. Some partners who had not witnessed began denying, so we disputed before a judge at Madina called Hisham ibn Isma'il. He ordered the slave-boy returned. I came to Urwah ibn al-Zubayr and told him, so he stood with me to him. Urwah said: Aishah, Mother of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with her, told me that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Yield is for liability. He said: So he retracted his judgment."
"14348","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, asked about that: he bought sheep and they grew, then came an order to annul the sale therein. He said: He returns the like of his sheep, and the growth is his, for the liability was upon him."
"14349","From al-Thawri, who said: When you buy sheep and they grow, then comes an order to annul the sale therein. He said: He returns them and their growth; and the slave-girl when she gives birth is like that."
"14350","Al-Thawri reported: Concerning wool, milk, and offspring: they are returned in the void sale when this was growth; it is returned in the goods. But dirhams and crops are not like it; if its principal perishes, then its value and the value of the growth, this in wool, milk, and offspring."
"14351","Ma'mar reported: A man from the people of al-Jazirah told me that he spoke to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz about a slave-girl seized from him. He said: So he returned her to me, and her growth."
"14352","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, who said: I heard him say: There is no liability upon the borrower, nor upon the non-treasonous trustee. I heard Hisham mention from Muhammad, from Shurayh the like of it, adding: The treasonous means the accused."
"14353","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: There is no liability upon the owner of the loaned item, nor upon the owner of the deposit, unless they violate."
"14354","Qays ibn al-Rabi' reported from al-Hajjaj, from Hilal, from Abd Allah ibn Ukaym al-Juhani, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: The loaned item is like the deposit, and there is no liability in it unless he transgresses."
"14355","Humayd reported from al-Hajjaj, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, that Ali ibn Abi Talib said: There is no liability upon the owner of the loaned item."
"14356","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, he said: He would not impose liability for the loaned item."
"14357","Israel reported, from Abd al-A'la, from Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah, from Ali, he said: The loaned item is not guaranteed; it is only a favor, unless he violates the terms, then he is liable. He said: And Abu Amir al-Sha'bi told me, he said: The borrower and the depositee bear no liability."
"14358","Ma'mar reported, from one of the sons of Safwan ibn Umayyah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) borrowed two items from Safwan, one with guarantee and the other without guarantee."
"14359","Ma'mar reported, he said: Qatadah used to say: Do not hold the borrower liable for the loaned item, unless its owner stipulates liability."
"14360","Israel reported, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay', from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, and he was a judge, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas: Shall I guarantee the loaned item? He said: Yes, if its owners wish."
"14361","Ibn Uyaynah reported, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The loaned item is compensated for. Amr said: And Ibn Abi Mulaykah told me, from Ibn Abbas, the same."
"14362","Al-Thawri reported, from Jabir, he said: I asked al-Hakam and al-Sha'bi about a man who borrowed a riding animal and hired it out for a dirham. Al-Hakam said: The dirham is his. And al-Sha'bi said: The dirham belongs to the owner of the animal."
"14363","Al-Thawri reported, he said: Every person who borrows something and pawns it with its owner's permission, and the pawn is lost, the borrower returns to the owner of the goods what he had pawned it for."
"14364","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, he said: O borrower of the pot, return it. And Ziyad said to me: O borrower of the pot, do not return it."
"14365","Ismail ibn Ayyash reported, from Shurahbil ibn Muslim, from Abu Umamah, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say in the Farewell Pilgrimage: The loaned item is to be returned, the granted milch animal is to be given back, debt is to be paid, and the guarantor is liable."
"14366","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said regarding the judgment of Mu'adh: Every loaned item is to be returned, and the guarantor is liable."
"14367","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that a man deposited eighty dirhams with his wife, and she moved the dirhams from her house, and they were lost, so he disputed with her before Shurayh. Shurayh said: Do you suspect her? He said: No. He said: If you wish, take fifty from her. He said: I never saw him order a settlement except on that day."
"14368","Ma'mar reported, from Qatadah, he said: There was a deposit with Anas ibn Malik, and it perished from among his wealth, so Umar ibn al-Khattab held him liable for it. Ma'mar said: Because Umar suspected him, saying: How was it lost from among your wealth?"
"14369","From al-Thawri, from Awf, from Anas ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, he said: Whoever is entrusted with a deposit and then entrusts it to another without its owner's permission has become liable."
"14370","Al-Thawri reported, from Jabir, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ali and Ibn Mas'ud, they said: There is no liability upon the trustee. Ma'mar said: I never heard anyone hold him liable; they say: He is trustworthy, unless treachery is found against him."
"14371","Al-Thawri reported, from al-Shaybani and Uthman al-Batti, from al-Sha'bi, he said: The deposit and the loaned item are like a debt."
"14372","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, he said: I asked Ibrahim about the deposit, and he said: It is like a debt when it is not identified."
"14373","Ma'mar reported, from Hammad, he said: I asked him about a man who died with a deposit in his possession and debt upon him, and the deposit was not distinguished from the debt. He said: They share by portions; he says: Whoever claims something from him shares in it."
"14374","Al-Thawri said regarding a man to whom a man said: I deposited this garment with you. He said: You spoke the truth. Then he said afterwards: Another man deposited it with me. He said: The garment belongs to the first, and he compensates the other with a garment."
"14375","Hisham reported, from al-Hasan, he said: If the depositee acts contrary to what he was ordered, he is liable, and if there is surplus in it, it is his by his liability. Hisham said: And al-Nakha'i said: It is not lawful for him."
"14376","Hushaym reported, from Sayyar, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Debt, profit-sharing partnership, and deposit, they are equally binding therein."
"14377","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: Whoever acknowledges something in his hands, his word is accepted. Ma'mar said: And al-Hasan and Uthman al-Batti said it."
"14378","He said: And Ibn Tawus said, and it is a man saying to a man: I had a deposit with you, then you returned it to me; he is believed if he had delivered it without evidence. And it is the saying of al-Hasan and Uthman al-Batti."
"14379","Waki' reported, from Ibn Abi Layla regarding a deposit handed to a man, he said: If it was handed to him sealed and its seal was broken and he took something from it, he is liable for it, otherwise there is no liability upon him. He said: And our companions said: He is liable only for what he spent."
"14380","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said regarding the executor: He is not removed unless he is suspected."
"14381","Al-Thawri reported, or another, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, he said: When the executor is suspected, he is removed, or another enters with him."
"14382","Ma'mar and al-Thawri reported, from Abu Ishaq, from his wife, that she entered upon Aishah with some women, and a woman asked her, saying: O Mother of the Believers, I had a slave girl, and I sold her to Zayd ibn Arqam for eight hundred until a term, then I bought her from him for six hundred, and I paid him the six hundred in cash and wrote eight hundred against him. Aishah said: By Allah, how evil what you bought, and by Allah, how evil what he bought! Inform Zayd ibn Arqam that he has nullified his jihad with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) unless he repents. The woman said to Aishah: What if I take my capital and return the excess to him? She said: Then whoever receives admonition from his Lord and desists, the verse. Or she said: And if you repent, yours is your capital, the verse."
"14383","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from his wife, she said: I heard the wife of Abu al-Safar say: I asked Aishah, saying: I sold a slave girl to Zayd ibn Arqam until the time of the stipend for eight hundred dirhams, and I bought her from him for six hundred. Aishah said to her: How evil what you bought, or how evil what he bought! Convey to Zayd ibn Arqam that he has nullified his jihad with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) unless he repents. She said: What if I take my capital? She said: No harm. Then whoever receives admonition from his Lord and desists, to him belongs what is past."
"14384","Ma'mar reported, from Amr ibn Muslim, he said: I asked Tawus about a man who sold goods to a man, can he buy it from him before paying him? He said: Some people permitted it, and some disliked it, and I dislike it."
"14385","Ma'mar reported, from Amr ibn Muslim, from Tawus, he said: Whoever buys goods on credit from a man, let him not sell it back to him; and whoever buys for cash, let him not sell it back to him on credit."
"14386","Ma'mar reported, he said: I asked Hammad about a man who bought goods from a man, can he sell it back to him before paying him at a discount? He said: No. And he disliked it until he pays him. From Ma'mar, from Amr ibn Muslim, from Tawus, the same as the saying of Hammad."
"14387","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: There is no harm in buying something for a term, then selling it to the one you bought it from for a lower price, when you settle accounts. And Ma'mar used to give fatwa by that."
"14388","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, and from Ibn Sirin, from a man, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, they said: When you sell a garment or a slave, and the term falls due, and you find it intact, and he says: Buy it from me, then buy it for what you sold it to him, or for less or more, so long as there is no credit involved."
"14389","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, and Ismail, from al-Sha'bi: They did not see any harm in the inah sale."
"14390","Hisham reported, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Beware lest there be silver for silver with a permissible item between them."
"14391","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: Ibn Umar was asked about a man who sold a saddle for cash, then wanted to buy it back for less than he had sold it before receiving payment. He said: Perhaps if he had sold it to another he would have sold it for less than that, so he saw no harm in it."
"14392","Ibn al-Taymi reported, from his father, he said: Hayyan ibn Umayr narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas say: When you sell silk garments on credit, do not buy them back."
"14393","Ja'far ibn Sulayman reported, he said: A cousin of mine told me that he asked Mujahid, saying: I sold silk to a man for a dinar until a term, and when the term came I found silk with him; shall I take it from him? He said: Do not take it except for more than you sold it to him for, if it is until a term; but if it has left his hand to another, there is no harm in buying it for whatever you wish."
"14394","I asked al-Thawri about a man who sells a riding animal for cash, then wants to buy it back for less than he sold it before receiving payment. He said: Al-Shaybani told me, from al-Sha'bi, and al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, that they both disliked it. He said: And Mansur told me, from Ibrahim, he said: If it has become lean and changed from its state, there is no harm in it. And al-Thawri used to give fatwa by it."
"14395","Ma'mar reported, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Shurayh sent goods with a man regarding a slave boy to Khurasan, but he did not buy him in Khurasan, saying: I left the like of this in Kufah, so he spent the goods on something else, and when he came to Kufah he bought for him. Shurayh asked the slave when he came: How did you find the man's company? He said: He bought me in Kufah. So Shurayh returned him to his owner and said: What about the liability from the River of Balkh?"
"14396","Ma'mar said, from Qatadah, regarding a man I ordered to buy for me for one hundred, but he bought for me for one hundred and ten, then it perished. He said: The excess of this one is gone, and the capital of this one. Ma'mar said: And I asked Ibn Shubrumah, and he said: The buyer guarantees all of it."
"14397","From al-Thawri, he said: When a man sends goods with a man for a garment, and he brings it as described but below its price, and it perishes, he is not liable."
"14398","From al-Thawri, he said: When a man says to a man: Buy for me a healthy slave, such and such, for one hundred dinars, and he finds that slave for fifty and buys him, he said: The buyer is not liable. But when he says: Buy for me a slave, such and such, for one hundred, and he finds two slaves for one hundred of that description, then it is not permissible for the first, and he guarantees the other."
"14399","Muhammad ibn Rashid reported, from Ibn Sirin, that Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman sent a man to buy for him two slave boys he had described to him, but he did not find as he had described to him, so he bought two slave boys and profited in them eight hundred dirhams. Hudhayfah said: Return to us our capital."
"14400","Al-Hasan ibn Umarah reported, he said: Shabib ibn Gharqadah and Ibn Arafah told us, from Urwah ibn Abi al-Ja'd al-Bariqi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me with a dinar to buy for him a sacrifice, then a man met me and I sold it to him for two dinars, then I bought for him another for a dinar, and I came to him with it and with the dinar, and I told him what I had done, and he prayed for me and blessed the transaction of my right hand. He said: I never bought anything except I profited in it. As for al-Thawri, he narrated from Abu Husayn, from a shaykh of the people of Madinah, from Hakim ibn Hizam, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him to buy for him a sacrifice, then he mentions the like of the hadith of Urwah ibn Abi al-Ja'd, except that Hakim said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave the dinar in charity."
"14401","Al-Thawri reported regarding a man who says to a man: Buy for me the slave of so-and-so. He says: Yes. Then he stands and buys him for himself: He belongs to the one who sent him, unless he said at the time of purchase: I bought him for myself."
"14402","Ibn al-Taymi reported, from a man, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, that he said regarding a man who orders a man to buy for him a slave girl for one thousand, but he buys her for one thousand five hundred. He said: If she dies on the road before he brings her, she is from the wealth of the buyer; and if she reaches the man, he has the choice, if he wishes he takes her, and if he wishes he leaves her."
"14403","He said: And al-Thawri said: When I order a man to buy for me a slave in Kufah and he buys him in Sanaa, then he is liable."
"14404","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from al-Hasan, he said: Sale cuts off hire. He said: And Ayyub said: It does not cut it off."
"14405","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Shubrumah, he said: I asked him about sale, does it cut off hire? He said: Yes."
"14406","Ibn Uyaynah reported, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, he said: A woman came to al-Sha'bi and said: Her sister apprenticed her slave boy among the masons for six months, and she died before the year. Al-Sha'bi held that the condition is broken if those who inherited the slave wish. Abd al-Razzaq said: Those who break the exchange."
"14407","From al-Thawri, he said: Sale and death cut off hire. As for death, al-Sha'bi judged by it. As for us, we say: In sale."
"14408","Ma'mar reported, from Hammad, he said: Whoever uses a slave without his masters' permission is liable. Al-Thawri reported, from Ash'ath, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, the same."
"14409","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, regarding the sale of clearance: when he sells it seeing it as his, then it is claimed after, he returns the sale to its owners and returns the capital to the buyer; and whoever sells while knowing it is not his is held to the purchase price. Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, that Iyas ibn Mu'awiyah judged in clearance like the saying of Tawus."
"14410","Ma'mar reported, from Tawus, from Mansur, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, that a woman and a son of hers sold a slave girl belonging to her husband, and the slave girl gave birth to the one who bought her, then her husband came and disputed before Ali, saying: I did not sell nor give. He said: Your son sold, and your wife sold. He said: If you see a right for me, give it to me. He said: Take your slave girl and her son. Then he imprisoned the woman and her son until they freed her for him, and when the husband saw that, he ratified the sale."
"14411","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, that Iyas ibn Mu'awiyah judged in the time of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, when a woman sold a house belonging to her husband while he was absent, and he came and said: My house, I did not sell, nor give, nor permit. So Iyas returned the house to her husband, then imprisoned her and said: Do not leave prison until you bring the like of this house in the like of this place. He said: I know only that he said: When the husband saw that, he ratified the sale."
"14412","Ma'mar reported, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about clearance in sale, and he said: This occurs in various ways. I said: What if a man sells something not his, then says to him: Its clearance is upon me? He said: This is nothing. Ma'mar said: I mentioned the saying of al-Zuhri to Ayyub, and he said: How good what he said."
"14413","From al-Thawri, from Mutarrif, from al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh, he said: Whoever stipulates clearance, deliver what you sold, or return what you took. Al-Thawri said: And he is not held to the purchase price in clearance."
"14414","Al-Thawri reported regarding a man who buys a house from a man, and he says to the buyer: I sell it to you when each of the partners permits, and for you the like of its measure from my other house. He said: The sale is valid, and his condition of the like of its measure is void."
"14415","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: Every condition in a sale, the sale is valid and the condition is void."
"14416","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, and al-Thawri, from Hisham, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, he said: When two authorised sellers sell, the sale belongs to the first. Ma'mar added in his hadith, he said: And he said regarding a man who sells goods to two men, he said: The sale belongs to the first of them; but if it is not known which of them he sold to first, it belongs to the one in whose hand it is."
"14417","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, regarding a man who sells to two men, he said: The sale belongs to the first, and to the latter the purchase price. Al-Thawri said: If it is not known which of them is first, it is rejected."
"14418","From al-Thawri, from Nusayr ibn Dhu'luq, from Umar ibn Rashid al-Ashja'i, he said: A man of the tribe sold a she-camel of his that had fallen ill, stipulating... then it recovered and he desired it, so they came to Umar ibn al-Khattab and told him the story. He said: Go to Ali and tell him the story. So they came to him, and he said: Take her and set her up in the market, and when she reaches her highest price, give him a price... from its price."
"14419","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that a man sold a cow stipulating its head, then he changed his mind and kept it, so Zayd ibn Thabit judged with the purchase of its head. Al-Thawri said: And we say: The sale is void."
"14420","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, he said: Aishah said: When Allah the Mighty and Majestic sent down the verses of usury from the end of Surah al-Baqarah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and recited them to us, then he forbade trade in wine."
"14421","Al-Thawri reported, from Abd al-A'la, from Suwayd ibn Ghaflah, he said: It reached Umar that his workers were taking wine in the jizyah, so he adjured them three times, and it was said: They do that. He said: Do not do it, but let them sell it, for the Jews were forbidden fats, so they sold them and ate their prices."
"14422","Ibn Uyaynah reported, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: It reached Umar that Samurah sold wine, and he said: May Allah fight Samurah; did he not know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah fight the Jews, fats were forbidden to them, so they melted them and sold them."
"14423","Ibn Uyaynah reported, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from a man, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I saw Umar turning his palm and saying: May Allah fight Samurah, a minor worker of ours in Iraq, he mixed into the fay' of the Muslims the price of wine and pork; it is unlawful and its price is unlawful."
"14424","Al-Thawri said regarding a Christian who lends a Christian in advance for wine, then one of them embraces Islam. He said: He has his capital; and when one of them lends the other wine, if the lender embraces Islam he takes nothing, and if the borrower embraces Islam he returns to the Christian the price of the wine."
"14425","Ma'mar reported, he said: I said to Ayyub: Shall I sell goods with a defect to one I know conceals it, with that defect? He said: What do you want, to sell only to the righteous?"
"14426","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever buys a sheep with milk retained in its udder, he milks it; if he is pleased with it he keeps it, otherwise he returns it with a sa' of dates."
"14427","Hisham reported, from Muhammad, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: Whoever buys a sheep with milk retained is given the choice for three days; if he returns it, he returns with it a sa' of dates."
"14428","Ma'mar reported, from one who heard al-Hasan, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever buys a sheep with milk retained, he milks it for three days; if he is pleased with it, otherwise he returns it with a sa' of dates."
"14429","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: Whoever buys a sheep with milk retained returns it with a sa' of dates."
"14430","Dawud ibn Qays reported, from Musa ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: Whoever buys a sheep with milk retained, if he milks it and is not pleased, he returns it with a sa' of dates."
"14431","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, raising it, he said: Whoever buys a sheep with milk retained, he milks it; if he is pleased with it he keeps it, otherwise he returns it with a sa' of dates."
"14432","Ma'mar reported, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: Abu Kathir told me that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you sells a sheep or a milch camel, let him not retain its milk."
"14433","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Khaythamah, from Abdullah, he said: Beware of animals with milk retained, for it is deceit, and deceit is not lawful for a Muslim."
"14434","Al-Taymi reported, from his father, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, he said: Whoever buys an animal with milk retained and returns it, let him return with it a sa' of dates."
"14435","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A townsman shall not sell for a Bedouin, do not outbid one another, a man shall not sell upon the sale of his brother, nor propose upon his proposal, and a woman shall not ask for the divorce of her sister to overturn what is in her vessel."
"14436","Abdullah ibn Umar reported, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man shall not sell upon the sale of his brother, nor propose upon his proposal, unless he permits him."
"14437","Ma'mar reported, from Hammam, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None of you shall sell upon the sale of his brother, nor propose upon his proposal."
"14438","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade meeting the riders and that a townsman sell for a Bedouin. I said to Ibn Abbas: What is his saying, a townsman for a Bedouin? He said: He shall not be a broker for him."
"14439","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from Ibn Sirin, from Anas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us that a townsman sell for a Bedouin, even if he is his father or his brother."
"14440","Al-Thawri reported, from Salih ibn Nabhan, that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A townsman shall not sell for a Bedouin."
"14441","From al-Thawri, from Abu Hamzah, from Ibrahim, he said: Umar said: Inform them of the price and direct them to the market."
"14442","Al-Thawri said, and Mughirah told me, from Ibrahim: They used to like to gain from the Bedouins in his saying: A townsman shall not sell for a Bedouin."
"14443","From al-Thawri, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from a man, from Khalid, with a lineage for him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave people, Allah provides some of them from others, and whoever consults his brother, let him advise him."
"14444","From al-Thawri, from Abu Musa, from al-Sha'bi, he said: The Emigrants used to dislike that, meaning a townsman selling for a Bedouin, yet we do it."
"14445","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Uthman, from Ata ibn Rabah, he said: I asked him about a Bedouin, shall I sell for him? And he permitted me."
"14446","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: He saw no harm in a townsman selling for a Bedouin."
"14447","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: Meeting the incoming goods was forbidden; so whoever meets incoming goods and buys from them, the seller has the choice when he reaches the market."
"14448","Ibn al-Taymi reported, from his father, from Abu Uthman, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade meeting the sales, or as he said."
"14449","Al-Thawri was asked: How far is the meeting? He said: When he goes out to where prayer is shortened, it is not meeting."
"14450","Ismail ibn Ayyash reported, he said: Amr ibn Muhajir al-Ansari told me, he said: I sold to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz a Muslim slave who sells captives, and when he finished, Umar said to him: How was the sale today? He said: It was dull, were it not that I raised upon them I would sell it. Umar said: You raised upon them without wanting to buy? He said: Yes. Umar said: This is najsh, and najsh is not lawful; send a caller to call that the sale is rejected and that najsh is not lawful."
"14451","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, from Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan, that he heard Ibn al-Khattab say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to withhold the maintenance of his family for a year, then he made what remained of his dates as the wealth of Allah."
"14452","Ma'mar reported, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from a man of the people of Syria, from Abu Dharr, he said: When my stipend comes out, I withhold from it the maintenance of my family; he said: Meaning until the next stipend comes out."
"14453","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: He used to have food from his land for two and three years, intending to sell it, waiting for high prices."
"14454","From al-Thawri and Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Ibn al-Musayyib: That he used to hoard oil."
"14455","Al-Aslami reported, from Abu Jabir al-Bayadi, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the sale of hoarding."
"14456","Ma'mar reported, from Qatadah, he said: He saw no harm in hoarding cloth."
"14457","From Ma'mar, he said: It reached me, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Ma'mar al-Adawi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None hoards except a sinner."
"14458","Ibn Jurayj and al-Aslami reported, from Abu Sa'id ibn Nubatah, from Nu'aym al-Mujmir, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that he said: If I saw Ma'mar ibn Abdullah al-Adawi while he says: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: None hoards except a sinner. Ibn al-Musayyib said: I said to him: But you hoard oil. He said: I seek Allah's forgiveness from it."
"14459","Al-Aslami reported, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None hoards except the treacherous, that is, the sinful wrongdoers."
"14460","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Abdullah ibn Babayh, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, he said: There is no man who sells food with no trade other than it except he is a sinner or a wrongdoer."
"14461","Al-Thawri said: We heard in some hadith: That the hoarder is cursed, and the importer is provided for."
"14462","Israel reported, from Ali ibn Salim, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The hoarder is cursed, and the importer is provided for."
"14463","Sufyan said: The hoarder is one who buys from the market, who purchases in the town; and one who imports, there is no harm in it, he is not a hoarder; and when he buys in the market and does not raise the price, there is no harm upon him."
"14464","Ismail ibn Ayyash reported, he said: Hurayz al-Rahbi told me, from Yunus ibn Sayf al-Absi, from Ka'b, that he used to say: Whoever withholds food forty nights to raise its price, then sells it and gives its price in charity, it is not accepted from him."
"14465","Ma'mar reported, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, he said: Prices rose once in Madinah, and people said: O Messenger of Allah, fix prices for us. He said: Allah is the Creator, the Provider, the Withholder, the Extender, the Price-Fixer; and I hope to meet Allah with none seeking from me an injustice I did to him in family or wealth."
"14466","From al-Thawri, from Ismail ibn Muslim, from al-Hasan, he said: It was said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Fix prices for us. He said: Allah is the Price-Fixer, the Appraiser, the Withholder, the Extender."
"14467","From al-Thawri, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, he said: It was said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Fix prices of food for us. He said: High prices and low prices are in Allah's hand, and I want to meet Allah with none seeking from me an injustice I did to him in wealth or blood."
"14468","From al-Thawri and Ibn Jurayj, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muslim ibn Jundub, he said: Food came to Madinah, and the people of the market went out to it and bought it. Umar said: ... Associate people, go out and travel, buy, then come and sell."
"14469","Ibn Uyaynah reported, from Kathir ibn Kathir ibn al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wada'ah, from Abdullah ibn Waqid ibn Abdullah ibn Umar, he said: Umar said: Whoever comes to our land with goods, let him sell them as he wishes, and he is my guest until he leaves, and he is our equal; and let no hoarder sell in our market. Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Umar, the like of it."
"14470","Ibn Jurayj and Muhammad ibn Muslim reported, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Whoever sells in our market, we guarantee him, and let no hoarder sell in our market."
"14471","Ma'mar reported, that it reached him that Umar passed by a man selling food who had lowered the price, and he said: Leave our market and sell as you wish."
"14472","Malik reported, from Yunus ibn Yusuf, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that Umar ibn al-Khattab passed by Hatib ibn Abi Balta'ah while he was selling raisins of his in the market, and Umar said to him: Either raise the price, or rise from our market."
"14473","Ibn Jurayj reported, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab found Ibn Abi Balta'ah selling raisins in Madinah, and he said: How do you sell, O Hatib? He said: Two mudds. He said: You buy at our doors, courtyards, and markets, cutting at our necks, then you sell as you wish; sell a sa', otherwise do not sell in our market; otherwise travel in the land and import, then sell as you wish."
"14474","Ma'mar reported, from Amr ibn Dinar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged regarding a runaway found in the Sanctuary with ten dirhams."
"14475","Hisham reported, from Muhammad, that Shurayh used to say: When found in the town, ten; and when found outside, forty dirhams. From al-Thawri, from Hisham, from Muhammad, from Shurayh, the like of it. From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh, the like of it. Al-Thawri said: And al-Hakam said: The Muslim returns for his brother."
"14476","From al-Thawri, from Abu Rabah, from Abu Amr al-Shaybani, he said: I came to Ibn Mas'ud with runaways I had found at al-Ayn, and he said: The reward and the spoil. I said: This is the reward, so what is the spoil? He said: Forty dirhams."
"14477","Ma'mar reported, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz judged for one day with a dinar, for two days two dinars, and for three days three dinars; what exceeds four, he has only four."
"14478","Al-Hasan ibn Umarah reported, from al-Hakam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ali, he said: Muslims return for one another."
"14479","From al-Thawri, from Ismail, from al-Sha'bi, he said: I heard him ask about a man who took a runaway slave and he fled from him. He said: There is no liability upon him."
"14480","From al-Thawri, from Yasir ibn Hazm, or Hazm ibn Yasir, from Jabir ibn al-Harith, he said: My master sent to me a slave he had taken in al-Sawad regarding whom celebration was made; the slave fled, so we disputed before Shurayh and he held him liable for him. We came to Ali and told him the story, and he said: Shurayh lied and judged badly; the black slave swears for the red slave, he fled a flight, and there is nothing upon him."
"14481","Ibn al-Taymi reported, he said: Ibn Abi Layla was asked about a man who took a runaway, and he fled from him. He said: If he took a wage he is liable, otherwise there is no liability upon him."
"14482","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, regarding a man who finds a foundling then spends upon him. He said: He has nothing of his spending; it is only something he sought reward for."
"14483","Ma'mar reported, from Qatadah, he said: Whoever revives an animal, it is his; he says: When its owners cast it away."
"14484","Sa'id ibn Bashir reported, from Qatadah, from al-Sha'bi, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever revives an animal, it is his."
"14485","From al-Thawri, from Dawud, from al-Sha'bi, regarding a man who finds an animal and feeds it. He said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz assigned him the fodder. Dawud said: And al-Sha'bi said: He has no replacement."
"14486","Yahya ibn al-Ala reported, from Mutarrif, from al-Sha'bi, asked about a man who lets loose an animal and a man takes it and repairs for it. He said: It was judged in this before today: if he let it loose in pasture and water, nothing; and if he let it loose in a desert and peril, the one who repaired to it is more entitled to it."
"14487","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: A slave boy of a man fled, and another knew his place and said: Sell me your slave boy, so he bought him from him, then disputed with him before Shurayh after that. I heard Shurayh say: Did you inform him of his place then buy him? And he rejected the sale because he had not informed him."
"14488","Yahya ibn al-Ala reported, from Jahdam ibn Abdullah, from Muhammad ibn Zayd, from Shahr ibn Hawshab al-Ash'ari, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade selling a slave while he is a runaway."
"14489","Abu Sufyan Waki' reported, from Zakariyya, from Amir, regarding a man who buys a runaway slave deceptively, whether he finds him or not. He disliked it and said: This is uncertainty. He said: And Wahb ibn Uqbah told me, he said: He has the choice when he finds him."
"14490","Ma'mar reported, from Hammad, he said: Whoever hires and transgresses and it perishes, he has the first hire, and liability is upon him; and if it is safe, nothing except the first hire. Ma'mar said: And Ibn Shubrumah said: He has the first hire, and liability, and the hire of what he transgressed."
"14491","Hisham reported, from Ibn Sirin: Regarding a man who hires a worker to carry on his back something to a known place, and he adds upon it; Shurayh fined him by the measure of what he added upon it by its reckoning."
"14492","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Shurayh imposed upon a man who transgressed by the measure of what he transgressed."
"14493","Al-Thawri reported, from Ash'ath, from al-Hakam, regarding the hirer who violates. He said: When it is safe, both hires gather upon him: the hire of what was timed, and the hire of what he added."
"14494","Al-Thawri said: When a man hires from a man and does not name what he carries and does not time, he said: He carries upon the animal what he wishes, and does not exceed what people see as carried; he may ride behind if he wishes, and gallop as people gallop; but if he names something he does not exceed it; and when he hires an animal and hires it to another, he is liable, even if like his condition."
"14495","Ma'mar said: When he hands it to a man and he carries upon it like his condition, he said: Nothing is upon him, and no liability."
"14496","Al-Thawri said, from al-Shaybani, from al-Sha'bi, he said: He is liable in what he violates, and there is no hire upon him."
"14497","From al-Thawri, regarding a man who hires a garment from a man each day for a dirham, and wears it a month except two days. He said: He takes from him the hire of the two days because he withheld its benefit, and the hire; and the animal is like that."
"14498","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Tawus, he said: My father used to make hire obligatory when a man goes out to Makkah, even if he dies before reaching. Ibn Tawus said: And I saw for the people of Madinah two hires: a hire with guarantee, and a hire without guarantee, which they stipulate; he says: If he dies, then my hire."
"14499","Ma'mar reported, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about a man who hires a camel, and the man dies on the road. He said: If the camel returns empty with nothing upon it, I see for him the measure of what his camel was ridden."
"14500","From al-Thawri, regarding a man who hires, and the hirer dies in part of the road. He said: It is by reckoning."
"14501","Al-Thawri reported: Al-Sha'bi was asked about a man who hires a riding animal to a place, but fulfills his need short of that place. He said: He owes hire in proportion to the distance he reached."
"14502","Sufyan said: If I say, I hire transport to such-and-such place for my food, and the carrier goes with him but does not load it on his camels, he said: He gets a comparable hire. Abd al-Razzaq said: I mentioned it to Ma'mar, and he said: He should satisfy him in proportion to the trouble he caused him."
"14503","From Al-Thawri, about a man who hires transport from a man to Makkah, with the carrier guaranteeing his maintenance until he arrives. He said: No, unless he fixes specified days and a specified measure of food which he gives him each day."
"14504","I heard Ma'mar asked about a man hiring an animal each day for so-and-so much. He said: There is no harm in it. He said to me: Ask about it in Makkah if you meet any of those people. So I performed Hajj and met only Hammad ibn Abi Hanifa, and asked him. He said: My father used to permit it, though it broke down for him by analogy. I said: Then why did he permit it. He said: Because it is the practice of the people. And he said: Whoever does not set aside analogy in the court session will not attain understanding."
"14505","From Al-Thawri, about a man who hires an animal until tomorrow. He said: It lasts until dawn breaks."
"14506","Hisham ibn Hassan reported from Muhammad, from Abu Hurayra: We were with him while he wore two dyed garments; he blew his nose then wiped his nose with his garment, and said: Praise be to Allah, Abu Hurayra blows his nose in linen. I saw myself falling between the pulpit of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the chamber of Aisha, unconscious from hunger, and a man would come and sit on my chest, and I would say: It is not that with me, it is only hunger. And he said: I was a hired worker for Ibn Affan and the daughter of Ghazwan, for a turn to ride on foot and the fill of my belly; I would serve them when they halted and drive their mounts for them when they departed. He said: She said one day: You shall surely mount it standing and bring it back on foot. He said: Then Allah married her to me, so I said: You shall surely bring it back on foot and mount it while it is standing. He said: And there was jest in him, meaning Abu Hurayra."
"14507","Ma'mar reported from Qatada, about a man who hires an animal from a man to a known land, then refuses to go out. He said: When it comes to his house and is detained there, the hire is not binding on him."
"14508","Ja'far reported from Hisham, from Al-Hasan, about a man hiring with guarantee. He said: I see no harm in it, when he pays him all his hire in advance, and his carrier equips him from his own wealth; and he disliked his hire being on credit."
"14509","Ma'mar reported: I asked Al-Zuhri about a man who hires transport from a man to Makkah and stipulates his maintenance upon him. He said: If he does not give him silver coins, there is no harm in it, when he gives him food."
"14510","Ma'mar reported from Qatada: He said: Every hired worker who takes a wage is liable if he wastes what is entrusted to him. Ma'mar said: And Ibn Shubruma said to me: He is liable only for what his hand spoils."
"14511","From Al-Thawri, from Abu Hamza, from Ibrahim: He said: Every joint hired worker is liable, except your private servant. He said: And Hammad used to hold nothing of this liable. Al-Thawri said: And Mutarrif reported from Al-Sha'bi: He is liable for what his hand spoils."
"14512","From Al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh, about a man who hires a man to work on his camel, and he beats the camel and puts out its eye. He said: He is liable for it."
"14513","Yahya ibn al-Ala informed us, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father: He said: Ali used to hold the tailor, the dyer, and the like liable, as a precaution for the people."
"14514","One of our companions reported to us, from Layth ibn Sad, from Talha ibn Abi Said, from Bukayr ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Ashajj: That Umar ibn al-Khattab held liable the dyer who works with his hand."
"14515","Al-Thawri reported from Jabir, from Al-Sha'bi: That Ali and Shurayh used to hold the hired worker liable."
"14516","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Shubruma: He said: I asked Ibn Hubayra and Ibn Abi Layla about a man who hires a ship and it breaks. I said: There is no liability on him. And Ibn Abi Layla said: The hired worker is liable. I said: Then if a thunderbolt from the sky strikes it and it burns. Ibn Hubayra considered it and said: There is no liability on him."
"14517","Ma'mar reported from Khalid al-Hadhdha: An elder of ours told me that he bought a large basin from a carpenter, and hired someone to carry it for him; a man carried it, and while he was walking a fragment met him. They disputed to Hisham ibn Hubayra, and he ruled against him for the basin."
"14518","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh: A man disputed to him, I think he said, about a fuller who tore a garment. Shurayh said: Whoever tears a garment, it is his and he owes one like it. A man said: Or its price. He said: It was dearer to him than its price on the day he bought it. He said: And is there no hadd. He said: No hadd. He said: What if they settle. He said: Then we will not quarrel between you."
"14519","Ma'mar reported from Qatada, about a man who tears a garment. He said: If it is worn out, he patches it; and if it is new, then its price."
"14520","From Al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Al-Sha'bi, from Masruq, about a fuller who tears a garment. He said: He is fined the deficiency in it, and returns it to the owner of the garment."
